the country;\n(b) if so, the estimated number of patients suffering from mental illnesses in the\ncountry;\n(c) whether the Government has made any assessment in this regard and if so, the\ndetails and the findings thereof;\n(d) whether the Government has made any assessment of such States which do not\nhave mental health hospital and if so, the details thereof, State -wise along with the\nreasons therefor; and\n(e) the corrective steps being taken by the Government in this regard\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(DR. HARSH VARDHAN)\n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 85* FOR 22ND NOVEMBER, 2019\n(a) to (e): The Government conducted the National Mental Health Survey through\nthe National Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences (NIMHANS),\nBangalore in 2016, as per which the prevalence of mental disorders in adults over\nthe age of 18 years is about 10.6%.\nPublic health and hospitals is a State subject and setting up of hospitals is\nprimarily within the domain of State Governments. Central Government, however,\nsupplements the efforts of State Governments. As per available information , there\nare 41 Mental Health Hospitals run by State Governments & Union Territory as per\ndetails at Annexure. Central Government is also running three Mental Health\nInstitutions, viz. National Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences,\nBangalore, Central Institu te of Psychiatry, Ranchi and Lokopriya Gopinath\nBordoloi Regional Institute of Mental Health, Tezpur, Assam. With a view to\nfurther increase mental healthcare facilities in the country, Department of\nPsychiatry has been set up in each of the 6 newly establ ished All India Institute of\nMedical Sciences (AIIMS) and similar provision has been made in the upcoming\n15 new AIIMS. Similarly, many other Central as well as State Government\nhospitals also have Psychiatry Departments. To strengthen mental health services at\nDistrict and Sub -District level, 655 districts of the country have been supported\nunder District Mental Health Programme (DMHP) of the National Health Mission.\n\nANNEXURE\nList of Government Mental Hospitals in the country\nS.No. State Name of the Mental Hospital\n1. Andhra\nPradesh\nGovernment Hospital for Mental Care, Chinnawaltair,\nVishakapatnam, Andhra Pradesh\n2. Bihar Institute of Mental Health, Koelwar, Bhojpur, BIHAR\n3. Jharkhand Ranchi Institute of Neuropsychiaty and Allied Science (RINPAS),\nKanke, Ranchi, Jharkhand\n4. Delhi Institute of Human Behavior & Allied Sciences, G.T. Road, P.O. Box\n9520, Jhilmil, Delhi\n5. Goa Institute of Psychiatry & Human Behavior, Panaji, GOA\n6.\nGujarat\nHospital for Mental Health, Bhuj, Gujarat\n7. Hospital for Mental Health, Vikasgruh Road, Jamnagar, Gujarat,\n8. Hospital for Mental Health, Behind Kapadia High School, Outside\nDelhi Gate, Shahibaug Road, Ahamedabad, Gujarat\n9. Hospital for Mental Health, Karelibag, Baroda\n10. Himachal\nPradesh\nHimachal Hospital of Mental Health & Rehabilitation, Boileauganj,\nShimla, HIMACHAL PRADESH\n11.\nJammu &\nKashmir\nGovt. Hospital for Psychiatric Diseases , Rainawari, Khatidarwaze,\nSRINAGAR (JAMMU & KASHMIR)\n12. The Medical Superintendent, Psychiatric Diseases hospital GMC,\nJammu, Ambphalla B.C. Road, Jammu, JAMMU AND KASHMIR\n13. Karnataka Karnataka Institute of Mental Health, Belgaum Road, Dharwad,\nKarnataka\n14.\nKerala\nMental Health Centre, Oolampara, Thiruvanathapuram, Kerala\n15. Govt. Mental Health Centre, Kuthiravattom, Kozhikode, Kerala\n16. Govt. Mental Health Centre, Poothole, Thrissur, Kerala\n17. Madhya\nPradesh\nGwalior Manasik Arogyasala, Central jail Road, Gwalior, Madhya\nPradesh\n18. Mental Hospital, Banganga Sawer Road, Indore, Madhya Pradesh\n19.\nMaharashtra\nRegional Mental Hospital, Nagpur, Maharashtra\n20. Regional Mental Hospital, Yeravda, Pune, Maharashtra\n21. Regional Mental Hospital, Wagle Estate, Thane (W) MAHARASHTRA\n22. Regional Mental Hospital, Ratnagiri, MAHARASHTRA\n23. Meghalaya Meghalaya Institute of Mental Health & Neurosciences, Shillong,\nMeghalaya\n24. Nagaland Mental Hospital, Kohima, Nagaland\n25. Orissa Mental Health Institute, S.C.B. Medical College, Cuttack, Orissa\n26. Punjab Dr. Vidyasagar Punjab Mental Hospital. Circular Road, Amritsar\n27.\nRajasthan\nMental Hospital (Psychiatric Centre), Janta Colony, Jaipur,\nRajasthan\n28. Mental Hospital, (Psychiatric Center), Shastri Nagar, JODHPUR\n\nS.No. State Name of the Mental Hospital\n29. Tamil Nadu Institute of Mental Health, Medavakkam Tank Road, Kilpauk,\nChennai, Tamil Nadu\n30. Telengana Institute of Mental Health, Govt. Hospital for Mental Care,\nHyderabad\n31. Tripura Modern Psychia"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|STARRED   |86", "t": "Green Alternatives of Plastics", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA  \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 86 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.11.2019 \n \nAGREEMENT WITH OTHER COUNTRIES  \n \n†86. SHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA: \n  \n Will the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPR ENEURSHIP be \npleased to state : \n \n(a)  whether the Government has signed any agreemen t with other countries to \ndevelop vocational skills in various sectors; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the specialization sectors for which agreement has been signed along with the \nname of the countries? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE \n MINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n \n(a) to (c) Ministry of Skill Development and Entrep reneurship has signed Fifteen (15) \nMemoranda of Understanding/Agreements/Joint Declara tion of Intent with Nine (9) \ncountries in the area of skill development and Vocational Education & Training, enhance \nand sharing of lessons learned in skill development, professional education and training, \nmutual recognition of qualifications to facilitate mobility of skilled work force, capacity \nbuilding and development of competence based curric ula etc. A Statement indicating \nthe name of countries with which this Ministry has signed various Memoranda of \nUnderstanding/Agreements/Joint Declaration of Inten t along with areas of collaboration \nis at Annexure . \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE  \n \nAnnexure as referred to in reply to parts (a) to (c ) of  Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo. 86 for answer on 18.11.2019 asked by Sh. Vishnu  Datt Sharma regarding \n“Agreement with other Countries”. \n \nS.No \n. \nName of \nthe \nCountry \nType of Agreement  Sectors covered  \n1. \n \nGermany \n \nJoint Memorandum of \nUnderstanding. \nCooperation in the field of Skill Development and \nVET, Dual apprenticeships and workplace-based \nskill development, Capacity Building including \nCompetency based curricula, Training of VET \ncluster managers etc. \nImplementation Agreement. Public and private sector s work together to \ncreate conditions conducive to improving \ncooperative vocational training in India. \nJoint Declaration of Intent  Training of VET Cluste r Managers and Indian \nSkill Development Officers that are directly \nengaged in the design and implementation of the \nprocesses of Dual VET in Clusters \n  Joint Declaration of Intent \non Skills for sustainable \ngrowth  \nVocational Education and Training \nJoint Declaration of Intent. \n \nSkill for sustainable growth  \n2   Japan  \n \nMemorandum of \nCooperation. \nTo train future shop floor leaders of Japanese \nstandard level in India with Japanese method of \nworking and to establish Japanese Endowed \nCourse (JEC) with existing engineering colleges \nin India to train future candidates for middle \nmanagement engineers in manufacturing sector. \nMemorandum of \nCooperation  \nTechnical Intern Training Programme (TITP) \n3 Switzerla \nnd  \nMemorandum of \nUnderstanding. \nSkills development, professional education and \ntraining. Sharing good practices in the areas of \nskill development. Capacity building of \nStakeholders through facilitating networks and \npartnership. \n4 United \nArabia \nEmirates \nMemorandum of \nUnderstanding  \nEnhance cooperation on Skill development and \nmutual recognition of cooperation to facilitate \nmobility of skilled workers from India to UAE.  \n\n5 Qatar Memorandum of \nUnderstanding  \nRecognition of professional and Trade \nQualifications in both countries. Recognition of \nexpertise of workers pre and post to the \nrecruitment process.  \n6 China Memorandum of \nunderstanding. \nTo strengthen technical cooperation through \nsharing experiences, best practices and \nknowledge in the field of skill Development.  \n7 Belarus Memorandum of \nUnderstanding  \nCurricula design, training and evaluation \nmethodologies, content, development, different \nlearning arrangements. \n8 UK \n \nImplementation Agreement  Promoting and expanding b ilateral relations in \nthe field of skill development.  \n \nMemorandum of \nUnderstanding  \nStrengthening skill delivery in high demand \nsectors where UK has technical and skilling \nexpertise, capacity building of institutions, \ntechnical assistance for a apprenticeships, \nquality assessment and certification. \n9 France  Memorandum of \nunderstanding  \nDevelopment of competence- based curricula \nand their dissemination within the training \nsystem. to build up capacities in training institutes \nand within micro, small and medium enterprises.  \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|STARRED   |87", "t": "Impact of Plastic Waste on Environment", "head": "��������\n����������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������������������\n�������������������������������������������������� \n����������������������������������������������������������\n��\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n�������������������������