 686.608-690.329 \nof NH -37 & construction of masonry drain.  \n(Job No.37/AS/2016-17/158) \n18.360 34.31 64.00 \n8 Strengthening of existing road, masonry drain from Km. 618.600 to \nKm. 633.600 (Length=15 Km) of NH -37  \n(Job No.37/AS/2016-17/159) \n15.000 28.20 2.00 \n9 Strengthening with construction of paved shoulder from Km. 138/00 to \nKm. 157/00 & Km. 171/00 to Km. 177/00 of NH -52 (NewNH -15)  \n(Job No.52 (New-15)/AS/2016-17/164) \n25.000 89.39 27.10 \n10 Strengthening with paved shoulder in Km. 13th to 15th, Km. 20th to \n23rd, Km. 35th, Km. 36th and Km. 57th to 63 of NH -15 (Total \nlength=16.870 Km) (Job No. 015/AS/2016-17/169)  \n16.870 92.34 68.74 \n\n \nAnnexure-I \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF THE UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 733 FOR ANSWER ON 21 st NOVEMBER, 2019 ASKED BY SHRI M. BADRUDDIN AJMAL \nREGARDING ROAD PROJECTS IN ASSAM. \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of Project Length \n(km) \nCost                       \n(Rs. in \nCrore) \nPhysical \nprogress                 \n(in %) \n11 Strengthening with paved shoulder from km 30/0 to km 46/0, km 54/0 \nto km 61/0, km 69/0 to km 80/0 and km  80/0 to km 96/0 of NH -39 for \nthe year 2015-16 including toll plaza.  \n(Job No.039-AS-2016-17-153) \n49.542 145.53 5 \n12 Strengthening with paved shoulder from Km. 858/000 to 862/000, Km. \n868/000 to 875/000, Km. 876/000 to 879/000, Km. 888/000 to 892/000, \nKm. 907/000 to 912/348 and Km. 912/780 to 915/000 including paved \nshoulder from Km. 862/000 to 868/000 and Km. 875/000 to 876/000 \nand Geometric improvement from Km. 871/825 to 872/200. \n(Job No.31/AS/2016-17/167) \n33.620 89.70 77.00 \n13 Strengthening and w"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |735", "t": "Promotion of Sports", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †735 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2019 \n \nPromotion of Sports \n \n†735.     SHRI NABA KUMAR SARANIA: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate: \na) whether any scheme is being implemented by the Union \nGovernment for the promotion of sports and also to provide financial \nassistance to the sportspersons, if so, the details thereof during the last \nthree years, State-wise; \n(b)  whether the Union Government proposes to bring any \nspecial scheme particularly for North -Eastern States as the people of \nthose areas have inclination towards sports and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(c) whether any proposal has  been received from the \nGovernment of Assam regarding promotion of sports, if so, the details \nthereof; \n(d) the schemes being implemented for BTC areas along with \nthe names of such areas where the Union Government proposes to \nconstruct new stadiums; and \n(e) the names of places where stadiums are being constructed \nduring the last three years for promotion of sports and the funds being \nspent on each stadium, State-wise? \n \n \n\nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[SHRI KIREN RIJIJU] \n \n(a):  Following schemes are being implemented by the Government for \nthe promotion of sports and also to provide financial assistance to the  \nsportspersons: \n(i)   Khelo India ; (ii) A ssistance to National Sports Federations ;                 \n(iii) Special Awards to Winners in International sports events and their \nCoaches; (iv) National Sports Awards; (v) Pension to Meritorious Sports \nPersons; (vi) Pandit Deendayal Upadhyay National Sports Welfare Fund; \n(vii) National Sports Development Fund; and (viii) running sports training \ncentres through Sports Authority of India. Details of these schemes are \nin the public domain  on the websites of this Ministry and Sports \nAuthority of India. Financial Assistance to athletes is given in the form \nof training and participation in competition at Sports Authority of India \nfacilities and through concerned National Sports Federations. Financial \nassistance is given to past high performers who are in indigent \ncircumstances directly through Direct Benefit Transfer  (DBT), the \ndetails of which are given at Annexure-I. \n \n(b):  All schemes of the Department of Sports are equally applicable to \nall parts of th e country without any discrimination to specific areas . \nThere is n o proposal to bring any  special sports scheme for North \nEastern States. In the North-Eastern States, the Department of Sports \nis running  National Sports University in Manipur  (Imphal) and \nLakshmibai National Institute of Physical Education in Assam \n(Guwahati).   \n \n\n(c):   Proposal for development of sports infrastructure in Guwahati has \nbeen received from the Government of Assam. In-principle approval for \ncentral assistance of Rs. 14.50 crore has been accorded. \n \n(d):    Department of Sports does not run any scheme to construct new \nstadium. However, u nder the existing BTC areas of Assam, SAI has \nestablished a SAI Training Centre at Kokrajhar where 136 Boys & 150 \nGirls were trained in the discipline of Archery, Athletics, Boxing, \nFootball, Hockey, Judo, Kabbadi, Karate, Taekwondo & Wushu in 2018-\n19. \n \n(e):   The Department of Sports does not have any scheme to construct \nstadium. During the last three years no new stadium was constructed \nby the Department of Sports.  \n \n***** \n \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nDetails of Sportspersons who have been given financial assistance from the fund of \n‘PanditDeendayaUpadhyay National Welfare Fund for Sportspersons’during each of the last \nthree years are as follows: \n \nYear  Name of \nsportsperson/ \norganization \nFinancial  \nassistance for \nState/UT to \nwhich \nbeneficiary \nbelongs \nAmount paid \n2016-17 Shri Mohd. Shahid,  \nFormer Hockey Player \nMedical Treatment Uttar Pradesh Rs. 10,00,000 \nShri Bir Bahadur \nSingh,  \nFormer Footballer \nAmeliorating \nfinancial condition \nTelangana Rs. 2,00,000 \nMsPreetiBeniwal For Medical \nTreatment \nHaryana Rs. 85,000 \n2017-18 Shri Mohammad \nHabib,  \nformer footballer \nMedical Treatment Andhra \nPradesh \nRs. 5,00,000 \nShri N.K. Mishra,  \nformer volleyball \nplayer \nAmeliorating \nfinancial condition \nRajasthan Rs. 5,00,000 \nShri Kaur Singh,  \nFormer Boxer \nAmeliorating \nfinancial condition \nPunjab Rs. 5,00,000 \nFamily of Late \nMsNitishaNegi, \nfootballer \nFinancial \nassistance to \nfamily \nDelhi Rs. 5,00,000 \nMsGohelaBoro, \nArcher  \nMedical Treatment Assam Rs. 3,37,500 \nFamily of Late Master \nPraveen, hockey \ntrainee at STC Bhopal \nFinancial \nassistance to \nfamily \nHaryana Rs. 2,00,000 \n2018-19 Shri N. Brij Kishore, \nGymnastic coach \nMedical treatment Telangana Rs. 6,00,000 \nFamily of Shri \nSumitRabha, football \nplayer from Assam \nMedical treatment Assam Rs. 5,00,000 \nMsKhaidemKalambia\nChanu, Fencing \nMedical treatment Assam Rs. 5,00,000 \nShri Laks"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |737", "t": "National Groundwater Management and Regulation Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 737\nANSWERED ON 21.11.2019\nNATIONAL GROUNDWATER MANAGEMENT AND REGULATION SCHEME\n737. SHRI COL. RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) the salient features of the National Groundwater Management and Regulation scheme along with the\nnames of the States where it has become operational;\n(b) whether the Bill for the conservation, protection, regulation and Management of Groundwater, 2016 is\nlikely to be re-introduced in this session and if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the status and details of aquifier mapping in the country, State-wise?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a) National Ground water Management and Regulation (GWM&R) Scheme is a Central Sector Scheme\nhaving following components:\n The National Aquifer Mapping and Management Programme (NAQUIM)\n Ground Water Monitoring, Resource Assessment, Regulation & Dissemination.\n Strengthening of Infrastructure for technological upgradation (Machinery & Equipment).\nThe scheme is operational in all the States / Union Territories of the country.\n(b) No Sir.\n(c) (NAQUIM) was initiated in the year 2012 as part of the Ground Water Management and Regulation\nscheme with the objective to delineate and characterize the groundwater aquifers and develop suitable\ngroundwater management plans. So far aquifer maps and management plans have been prepared for nearly\n11.24 lakh square-km area spread over various parts of the country. State/UT-wise areas covered are given\nin the Annexure.\n*****\n\nAnnexure\nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (c) of Unstarred Question No. †737 to be answered in\nLok Sabha on 21.11.2019 regarding “ National Groundwater Management & Regulation\nScheme”.\nState and UT-wise areas (in km2) for which aquifer maps have been prepared and management plans\nhave been developed since the inception of the programme.\nS.No. State/UT Area for which aquifer maps and management\nplans have been prepared (km2)\n1. Andaman and Nicobar Islands 800\n2. Andhra Pradesh 64185\n3. Arunachal Pradesh 3399\n4. Assam 13980\n5. Bihar 25657\n6. Chandigarh 115\n7. Chhattisgarh 29616\n8. Dadra & Nagar Haveli 490\n9. Delhi 1483\n10. Goa 3702\n11. Gujarat 57956\n12. Haryana 44179\n13. Himachal Pradesh 5840\n14. Jammu & Kashmir 9506\n15. Jharkhand 29241\n16. Karnataka 91896\n17. Kerala 18166\n18. Lakshadweep 32\n19. Madhya Pradesh 77970\n20 Maharashtra 86863\n21. Manipur 1655\n22. Meghalaya 8678\n23. Mizoram 700\n24. Nagaland 910\n25. Odisha 24912\n26. Pudducherry 454\n27. Punjab 50368\n28. Rajasthan 183747\n29. Sikkim 280\n30 Tamil Nadu 87174\n31. Telangana 48963\n32. Tripura 6757\n33. Uttar Pradesh 103928\n34. Uttarakhand 7811\n35. West Bengal 32631\nTotal 1124044\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |738", "t": "Kuttanad Inland Faring Region", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 738\nANSWERED ON 21.11.2019\nKUTTANAD INLAND FARING REGION\n738. SHRI KODIKUNNIL SURESH\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Union Government has allocated and released any funds for the purpose of strengthening\nof the outer bunds of Kuttanad inland faring region in Kerala State during the last three years;\n(b) if so, the quantum of funds allocated and released for the purpose, year-wise;\n(c) whether the Union Government has received any request from the Government of Kerala in this\nregard;\n(d) if so, the details thereof along with the response of the Union Government thereon;\n(e) whether the Union Government is aware that the strengthening of outer bunds is necessary to protect\nthe paddy farming in Kuttanad which is a below mean sea level farming zone; and\n(f) if so, the details of action taken in this regard so far?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a ) & (b) Four Projects in the Kuttanad region of Kerala were approved during XI Plan under Flood\nManagement Programme (FMP) of the Ministry. The details of Central Assistance (CA) released during\nlast three years is as under:\nS. No. Scheme Central Assistance released in\nRs. Crores\nTotal Central\nAssistance\nreleased till\ndate in Rs.\nCrores\n2016-17 2017-18 2018-19\n1. KEL-1\nMitigation of Flood in Group-I\n(Padasekharams) in Kuttanad region\n- 1.29 11.20\n2. KEL-2\nRegulation of flood water in Kayal area, 4\npadesekharams and Mitigation of floods in\ngroup 9, 5 nos. Padasekharams in Kuttanad\nregion\n- - - 65.86\n3. KEL-3\nMitigation of floods in Kuttanad region –\nPhase-I (group 2-5, 7, 8, 10-19), 231\npadasekharams (paddy fields)\n- 17.76 - 49.64\n4. KEL-4\nMitigation of floods in Onattukara region\ncomprising of 12 watersheds in Kerala State\n- - - 11.25\nContd…P/2\n\n//2//\n(c) & (d) No proposal for release of outstanding Central Assistance in respect of these four projects, as\nper extant guidelines of FMP, has been received from the State Government of Kerala in this Ministry, in\nthe current Financial year 2019-20.\n(e) & (f) Union Government is aware that the strengthening of outer bunds is necessary to protect the\npaddy farming in Kuttanad which is below mean sea level farming zone. The flood management schemes\nare formulated and implemented by concerned States as per t heir priority. The Union Government\nsupplements the efforts of the States by providing technical advice and promotional financial assistance\nfor management of floods in critical areas. Considering the situation in Kuttanad area above four projects\nwere approved under FMP of Union Government wherein, outer bunds were strengthened in an area about\n16000 hectares.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |739", "t": "Irrigation Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 739\nANSWERED ON 21.11.2019\nIRRIGATION PROJECTS\n739. SHRIMATI PRAMILA BISOYI\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) the action plan for creating additional irrigation facility by providing budgetary support to States during\nthe financial year 2019-20;\n(b) the total number of major irrigation projects supported through budget for the said year, State -wise and\nplan of action to complete these projects; and\n(c) the total number of medium irrigation projects taken up during the last five years across the States\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a) & (b) The Water Resources Projects are planned, funded, executed and maintained by the State\nGovernments themselves as per their own resources and priority. In order to supplement their efforts,\nGovernment of India provides technical and financial assistance to State Governments to encourage\nsustainable development and efficient management of water resources through various schemes and\nprogrammes such as Accelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme (AIBP ) etc. under Pradhan Mantri Krishi\nSinchayee Yojana (PMKSY).\nDuring 2016-17, ninety-nine (99) on-going Major/Medium irrigation projects (and their 7 phases)\nunder PMKSY-AIBP having ultimate irrigation potential of 76.03 lakh hectare and balance estimated cost\nof Rs.77595 crore [Central Assistance (CA) component of Rs.31342 crore] have been prioritized in\nconsultation with States for completion in phases up to December, 2019. Funding mechanism through\nNABARD has also been appr oved by the Government for both Central and State share of all prioritized\nprojects. CA is released from time to time as per requirement. Out of above projects, 66 projects (and 7\nphases) are Major projects. State wise details of these Major projects incl uding CA released during 2019 -\n20 are at Annexure.\nFurther, Government of India has also approved the funding of “Implementation of Shahpurkandi\nDam (National Project) on River Ravi in Punjab State” w ith an estimated cost of the Rs. 2715.70 crore\n[ Irrigation Component: 776.96 crore]. Central Assistance for the balance works portion of\nContd…P/2\n\n//2//\nIrrigation Component is Rs.485.38 crore . Funding for CA to the project has been arranged through\nNABARD under LTIF.  The project envisages to provide irrigation in 5000 ha and 32173 ha in the State of\nPunjab and J&K respectively. During 2019-20, CA of Rs 60 crore has been sanctioned for this project.\nFurther, the Union Cabinet has approved the “Relining of Sirhind Feeder from RD 119700 to\n447927 and Rajasthan Feeder from RD 179000 to 496000” on 26.09.2018. The approved cost of these\nprojects is Rs.1976.75 crore (CA component: Rs.826.168 c rin addition to Rs.156 crore of CA released\nearlier for these projects). Funding of CA for these projects has been arranged through NABARD under\nLTIF. The relining of Rajasthan Feeder envisages stabilization/ improvement of irrigation in 98,739 Ha of\narea in Rajasthan. Relining of Sirhind Feeder envisages stabilization/ improvement irrigation in 69,096 Ha\nof area (20,740 Ha in Rajasthan and 48,356 Ha in Punjab).\nThe Polavaram Irrigation Project (PIP) has been declared as a National Project as per Section 90\nof AP Reorganization Act, 2014. Central Government is funding 100% of the remaining cost of the\nirrigation component only of the pro ject for the period starting from 01.04.2014 to the extent of the cost of\nthe irrigation component on that date. Ultimate Irrigation Potential of the project is 2.91 Lakh Ha. CA of\nRs.562.47 crore has been released for this project under AIBP up to 2013-14 and CA of Rs.6764.16 crore\nhas been released for this project as National Project after 01.04.2014.\nA Special Package for completion of Irrigation Projects to address agrarian distress in Vidarbha,\nMarathwada and other chronically droug ht prone areas of Rest of Maharashtra has been approved during\n2018-19. Under the Special Package, 8 MMI projects and 83 SMI projects with a balance cost of\nRs. 13,651.607 crore as on 01.04.2018 are planned to be completed in a phased manner up to 2022-23. The\ntargeted additional potential through these projects is 3.77 LHa. CA is being provided through Budgetary\nsupport. During 2018 -19, and 2019 -20 (so far) Rs.500 crore and Rs.295 crore have been released\nrespectively as CA under the Special Package.\nSurface Minor Irrigation (SMI) scheme and Repair, Renovation and Restoration (RRR) of\nWater Bodies are also being implemented under Har Khet Ko Pani (HKKP) component of PMKSY.\nCentral Assistance (CA) to SMI and RRR of Water Bodies schemes is released through budgetary support.\nThe Budget provision of Rs 750 crore has been made for these schemes during 2019 -20. CA of Rs.648.29\ncr has been released during 2019-20 so far.\n(c) From 2014-15 onwards, 58 numbers of Medium"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |74", "t": "Students-Teachers Ratio in EMRS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *74\nANSWERED ON 21.11.2019\nINTER-LINKING OF RIVERS\n*74. SHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS\nSHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether any proposals regarding inter-linking of rivers in Karnataka, Rajasthan, Maharashtra and Uttar\nPradesh are pending with the Government;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor;\n(c) the details of work done so far to inter-link the waters of rivers and dams in the said States; and\n(d) whether any hindrances are being faced in completion of the said projects and if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI\n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT)\n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF STARRED QUESTION\nNO.*74 TO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2019 RE GARDING “INTER-LINKING OF RIVERS ”\nRAISED BY SHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS & SHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA. M.Ps.\n(a) to (c ) The National Perspective Plan (NPP) was prepared by the then Ministry of Irrigation (now\nMinistry of Jal Shakti) in August 1980 for water resources development through inter basin transfer of\nwater, for transferring water from water surplus basins to water -deficit basins. Under the NPP, the\nNational Water Development Agency (NWDA) has identified 30 links (16 under Peninsular Component\nand 14 under Himalayan Component) for preparation of Feasibility Reports (FRs).\nUnder NPP, three inter -linking of rivers (ILR) projects namely, Krishna (Almatti) -Pennar link\nproject, Bedti -Varda link project and Netravati -Hemavati link project have been identified benefitting\nKarnataka State.\nThree ILR projects namely, Yamuna- Rajasthan link project, Rajasthan-Sabarmati link project and\nParbati–Kalishindh – Chambal link project have been identified benefitting Rajasthan State.\nTwo ILR projects namely, Damanganga -Pinjal link project and Par -Tapi-Narmada link project\nhave been identified benefitting Maharashtra State.\nSix ILR projects namely, Ken -Betwa link project, Gandak - Ganga link project, Ghaghra\n(Karnali)-Yamuna link project, Sarda -Yamuna link project, Chunar -Sone Barrage link project and Kosi -\nGhaghra (Karnali) link project have been identified benefitting Uttar Pradesh State.\nThe details of the above ILR projects indicating their status is given at Annexure-I.\nApart from the above, NWDA has received proposals of 6 intra -State link projects from\nGovernment of Karnataka, 20 intra -State link projects from Government of Maharashtra and 2 intra -State\nlink projects from Government of Rajasthan.\nThe details of the above intra-State link projects indicating their status is given at Annexure-II.\n(d) The implementation of river link projects involves various steps such as preparation of Pre Feasibility\nReports/ Feasibility Reports of links, negotiation and consensus among concerned States, preparation of\nDetailed Project Reports (DPRs) of the projects, clearances from Ministry of Environment and Forests and\nClimate Change, Ministry of Tribal Affairs, techno -economic clearance by Advisory Committee on\nIrrigation, Flood control & multipurpose projects of Ministry of Jal Shakti, investment clearance.\nThe stage of implementation of a project would be reached after its DPR is prepared with the\nconsensus of concerned States and the requisite statutory clearances are obtained.\nThe main problems involving implementation of the ILR projects include problem in consensus\namong party States, funding for implementation, implementation mechanism and international issues\n(projects involving other countries).\n*****\n\nANNEXURE-I\nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (c) OF STARRED QUESTION NO. *74 TO\nBE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 21.11.2019 REGARDING “INTER-LINKING OF RIVERS”.\nNames of Inter Basin Water Transfer Links, name of rivers, status of Feasibility Reports/Detailed Project\nReport\nSl.\nNo.\nName Rivers Present Status of Project\nProjects benefitting Karanataka State\n1. Krishna\n(Almatti) –\nPennar link\nKrishna  &\nPennar\nFR Completed\n2. Bedti - Varda\nlink\nBedti & Varda PFR Completed\n3. Netravati –\nHemavati link\nNetravati &\nHemavati\nPFR Completed\nProjects benefitting Rajasthan State\n1. Yamuna-\nRajasthan link\nYamuna &\nSukri\nDraft FR  completed\n2. Rajasthan-\nSabarmati link\nSabarmati Draft FR  completed\n3. Parbati -\nKalisindh-\nChambal link\nParbati,\nKalisindh &\nChambal\nFR Completed\nRecently Govt. Of Rajasthan has proposed integration of Eastern\nRajasthan Canal Project with Parbati -Kalisindh- Chambal link.\nProjects benefitting Maharashtra State\n1. Damanganga –\nPinjal link\nDamanganga &\nPinjal\nFR & DPR Completed.\nTechno-economic clearance accorded subject to Forest clearance\nfor diversion of 1674 ha of forest land and clearance for R&R of\nTribal population from MoTA.\n2. Par-Tapi-\nNarmada link\nPar, Tapi &\nNarmada\nFR & DPR Completed.\nDPR sent to CWC for technical appraisal.\nCombined MoU for impl"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |740", "t": "Road Development Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 740 \nANSWERED ON 21st NOVEMBER, 2019  \n \nROAD DEVELOPMENT PROJECTS \n \n740. SHRI DHARAMBIR SINGH: \n \n \nWill the minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether many road development projects are either running behind schedule or have been \nstopped in the country especially in Haryana; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, project- wise along with the reasons therefor; \n(c) the steps taken to complete these projects at the earliest; \n(d) whether the Government has identified any fault at the level of Government agencies \nengaged in formulation of scheme and development of road projects; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the remedial measures taken/being taken by the Government in \nthis regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) to (c) There are 566 National Highways projects running behind their schedule and out of \nwhich, 12 nos. projects are in Haryana. These projects are delayed mainly due to land \nacquisition, utility shifting, non -availability of soil/aggregate, poor performance of contractors, \nenvironment/forest/wildlife clearances, ROB & RUB issues with Railw ays, public agitation for \nadditional facilities, arbitration/contractual disputes with contractors etc. To fast -track these \nprojects, various steps such as streamlining of land acquisition &environment clearances, \npremium re -scheduling, close coordination with other Ministries, revamping of dispute \nresolution mechanism, frequent review meetings with project developers, State Government and \ncontractors at various levels etc. have been taken up. \n \n(d) & (e) Though projects get delayed due to various reasons such as land acquisition, \nutility shifting, environment/forest/wildlife clearances, ROB/RUB issues with the railways, \nhowever to overcome these, it has been now decided that projects are awarded only when 90% of \nthe land required is available and all the statutory clearanceshave been obtained. During \nexecution stage, r egularmeetings at higher level are held time to time to monitor the ongoing \nprojects. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |741", "t": "JNNSM", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 741 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11. 2019 \n \nJNNSM \n741. SHRI N. REDDEPPA \n        SHRI PASUNOORI DAYAKAR \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is implementing any scheme under Jawaharlal Nehru National \nSolar Mission (JNNSM); \n(b) if so, the details thereof for the last three years, State/UT-wise; and \n(c) the steps being taken to promote and encourage small solar power projects in rural areas?   \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (I/C) FOR NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY,  POWER and MoS \nfor  SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R. K. SINGH) \n \n(a) and (b): T he Union Government has been implementing National Solar Mission under \nwhich various Schemes as detailed in Annexure-I have been launched. The state-wise details \nof cumulative installed solar power capacity during last three years are given in Annexure-II. \n(c) The Government of India has launched a new Scheme ‘Pradhan Mantri - Kisan Urja Suraksha \nevam Utthaan Mahabhiyan (PM -KUSUM)’ to enhance energy independence and income of \nfarmers. The scheme, inter alia, provides for: \n(i) setting up of grid -connected renewable energy power plants each of capacity upto 2 \nMW by farmers; \n(ii) installation of 17.5 lakh standalone off-grid solar water pumps by 2022; \n           (iii) solarization of 10 lakh existing grid-connected agriculture pumps by 2022. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART S (A) AND (B) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 741 FOR 21.11.2019 \nDETAILS OF SCHEMES LAUNCHED UNDER NATIONAL SOLAR MISSION \ni. Solar Park Scheme for setting up of over 50 Solar Parks and Ultra Mega Solar Power Projects \ntargeting over 40,000 MW of solar power projects.  \nii. Scheme for setting up 1000 MW of Grid -Connected Solar PV Power Projects by the Central \nPublic Sector Undertakings (CPSUs) and the Government of India organisations with Viability \nGap Funding (VGF).  \niii. Scheme for setting up 300 MW of Grid -Connected Solar PV Power Projects by Defence \nEstablishments and Para Military Forces with VGF.  \niv. Pilot-cum-demonstration projects for development of grid connected solar PV power plants \non canal banks and canal tops. \n v. Bundling Scheme - 15000 MW grid -connected solar PV power plants through NTPC Ltd./ \nNVVN. \n vi. VGF Scheme for setting up of 2000 MW of Grid Connected Solar PV Power Projects \nthrough SECI.  \nvii. VGF Scheme for setting up of 5000 MW of Grid Connected Solar PV Power Projects \nthrough SECI.  \nviii. Installation of Grid Connected Solar Rooftop Power Plants. \nix. Off-Grid Solar PV Scheme. \nx. Pradhan Mantri - Kisan Urja Suraksha evam Utthaan Mahabhiyan (PM-KUSUM). \n***** \n  \n\n                                                                                                                                \n                                                                                                                                                             ANNEXURE-II \nANNEXURE-II REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART S (a) and (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 741 FOR 21.11.2019 \nSTATE-WISE DETAILS OF CUMULATIVE GRID CONNECTED INSTALLED SOLAR POWER \nCAPACITY DURING LAST THREE YEARS \n(In MW) \nSr. No. State/UT Capacity Added \nduring  \n2016-17  \n Capacity Added \nduring  \n2017-18 \nCapacity Added \nduring  \n2018-19   \n1 Andaman & Nicobar 1.46  0.00  5.17  \n2 Andhra Pradesh 1294.26  328.24  890.22  \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 0.00  5.13  0.00  \n4 Assam 11.78  0.67  9.95  \n5 Bihar 103.42  33.93  0.00  \n6 Chandigarh 10.52  7.88  9.51  \n7 Chhattisgarh 35.28  102.49  0.00  \n8 Dadar &Nagar  2.97  2.49  0.00  \n9 Daman & Diu 6.46  0.15  3.86  \n10 Delhi 25.99  17.80  57.32  \n11 Goa 0.71  0.20  2.98  \n12 Gujarat 130.19  328.89  852.13  \n13 Haryana 66.01  123.45  7.67  \n14 Himachal Pradesh 0.53  0.00  21.95  \n15 J&K 0.36  0.00  13.47  \n16 Jharkhand 7.08  2.39  9.28  \n17 Karnataka 882.38  3915.22  1151.43  \n18 Kerala 61.15  33.46  30.65  \n19 Lakshadweep 0.00  0.00  0.00  \n20 Madhya Pradesh 80.67  448.31  534.81  \n21 Maharashtra 66.61  777.56  394.36  \n22 Manipur 0.03  0.03  3.38  \n23 Meghalaya 0.01  0.01  0.10  \n24 Mizoram 0.00  0.10  0.30  \n25 Nagaland 0.50  0.50  0.00  \n26 Odisha 12.50  0.15  315.16  \n27 Pondicherry 0.05  0.08  2.98  \n28 Punjab 388.89  104.15  0.00  \n29 Rajasthan 543.00  518.49  894.02  \n30 Sikkim 0.00  0.00  0.01  \n31 Tamil Nadu 630.01  213.65  666.65  \n32 Telangana 759.13  2004.27  300.84  \n33 Tripura 0.09  0.00  0.00  \n34 Uttar Pradesh 193.24  357.13  265.69  \n35 Uttarakhand 192.35  24.79  46.67  \n36 West Bengal 18.37  11.06  38.63  \nTOTAL 5525.98  9362.63  6529.20  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |744", "t": "Construction Work of NH in Maharashtra", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 744 \nANSWERED ON 21ST NOVEMBER, 2019 \n \nCONSTRUCTION WORK OF NH IN MAHARASHTRA \n744. SHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL:  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government is aware that the construction work on Ratnagiri -Nagpur, \nGuhagar-Vijapur, Manmad -Chikkodi highway is progressing at a very slow pace and if so, the \nreasons therefor; \n(b) whether the Govern ment has issued necessary directions to the concerned contractors and \nofficials to expedite the work thereon and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether any action is likely to be taken against the contractors who are lagging behind \nschedule in completion of the assigned project; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) & (b)  The road from Ratnagiri  to Nagpur comprises 4 different NHs (NH -166, NH-211,NH-\n361 and NH -7) and 16 different project sub -sections. The road from Guhagar to Vijapur is NH \n166E comprising of 6 different project sub -sections. The road from Manmad to C hikkodi has two \ndifferent rout e options along National Highways. First route is from Manmad to Chikkodi via \nPimpalgaon-Nashik-Pune-Miraj-Chikkodi, comprising of 5 different NHs (NH 752G, NH 60, NH \n48, NH 166 and NH 160) and 8 different project sub -sections. The second route is from Man mad \nto Chikkodi via Kopargaon -Ahmednagar-Vasundhephata-Dahiwadi-Miraj-Chikkodi, comprising \nof 3 different NHs (NH 752G, NH 160B and NH 160) and 5 different project sub -sections. The \ndetails of ongoing works in above project stretches are annexed below. Som e of the above project \nstretches were initially delayed owing to land acquisition, utility shifting & slow mobilization by \nthe Contractor. These projects have been periodically reviewed at various levels in Ministry and \nnecessary directions have been issued to all concerned including Contractor and officials to initiate \nnecessary action for expediting the progress of works. \n(c) & (d)  The projects are reviewed periodically and if the reason for delay is attributable to the \nContractor, the action as per provisions of Contract Agreement are being taken. \n \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXUR EREFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 744 FOR ANSWER ON 21.11.2019 ASKED BY  SHRI SANJAY KAKA \nPATIL REGARDING CONSTRUCTION WORK OF NH IN MAHARAHSTRA  \n(i) Status of road from Ratnagiri – Nagpur: \nS. \nNo Section NH \nNo. \nLength \n(km)  Status/ Remarks \n1 Ratnagiri - \nKolhapur 166 133.000 In DPR stage \n2 Kolhapur - Sangli 166 35.000 This section is with State Government and \ndeveloped under BOT. \n3 \nSangli – Solapur \nPkg-I (Sangli to \nBorgaon)  \n166 41.444  \n4- laningproject work under implementation \nstage,targeted for completion within scheduled \ndate of completion. \n4 \nSangli – Solapur \nPkg-II (Borgaon to \nWatambare) \n166 52 \n5 \nSangli – Solapur \nPkg-III \n(Watambare to \nMangalwedha)   \n166 45.600 \n6 \nSangli – Solapur \nPkg-IV \n(Mangalwedha to \nSolapur) \n166 56.500 \n7 Solapur - Tuljapur 211 39.000 \n4-laning completed as a part of Solapur – \nYedishi BOT (Toll) Project. \n8 Tuljapur - Ausa 361 67.428 \n4- laningproject work under implementation \nstage,targeted for completion within scheduled \ndate of completion. \n9 Ausa – Chakur –  361 58.51  \n4- laningproject work under implementation \nstage, Cure period notice has been given. 10 Chakur – Loha 361 73.345 \n11 Loha - Waranga 361 56.569 \n12 Waranga – \nMahagaon 361 66.880 4- laningproject work under implementation \nstage,targeted for completion within scheduled \ndate of completion. 13 Mahagaon – \nYavatmal 361 80.195 \n\n14 Yavatmal – \nWardha 361 64.925 \nProvisional completion for 4-laning project \nwork issued.Targeted forcompletion as per \nscheduled date. \n15 Wardha-Butibori 361 59.190 \n4- laningproject work under implementation \nstage,targeted for completion within scheduled \ndate of completion. \n16 Butibori - Nagpur 7 28.800 Project work of 4-laning completed. \n(ii) Status of road from Guhagar-Vijapur: \nSr. \nNo. Section NH No \nTotal \nLength \n[km] \n \nPresent status \n1 Guhagar-Chiplun 166E 39.83 \n2 lane with Paved shoulder \nwork under implementation stage \nwith progress of 16%. \n2 Chiplun-Helwak 166E 46.57 2 lane with Paved shoulder \nwork under bidding stage. \n3 Helwak-Karad 166E 48.417 \n2 lane with Paved shoulder \nwork under implementation stage \nwith progress of 35%. \n4 Karad-Vita 166E 47.5 \n2 lane with Paved shoulder \nwork under implementation stage \nwith progress of 36%. \n5 Vita-Nagaj 166E 46.655 \n2 lane with Paved shoulder \nwork under implementation stage \nwith progress of 14%. \n6 Nagaj-Jath-Vaijapur 166E 49.381 \n2 lane with Paved shoulder \nwork under implementation stage \nwith progress of 67%. \n  \n\n(iii) Status of road from Manmad-Chikkodi: \n First Route: \nSr. \nNo. Section NH No Present Status \n1 Manmad – Chandwad – \n2L+"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |747", "t": "Allocation of Power to States", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.747 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2019 \n \nALLOCATION OF POWER TO STATES \n \n747. SHRIMATI VANGA GEETHA VISWANATH: \nSHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has any details of the total quantum of \npower generated by the Central Power Generating Stations; \n \n(b)  if so, the details of the allocation of power from such stations to \neach State during each of the last three years; \n \n(c)  whether the Government would like to enhance the quota of States \nwhere there is need; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof along with the action taken thereon? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER, NEW & \nRENEWABLE ENERGY AND THE MINISTER OF STATE FOR SKILL \nDEVELOPMENT & ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n( SHRI  R.K. SINGH)  \n \n(a) :  Details of power generation from fuel sources (Thermal, Hydro \n& Nuclear) of 25 MW and above by the Central Generating Stations in the \ncountry during the last three years and current year i.e. from 2016 -17 to \n2019-20 (upto October, 2019) is given at Annexure-I. Also, details of \nRenewable Energy Generation data of Central Public Sector Undertakings \n(CPSUs) Stations available in Central Electricity Authority (CEA) for year \n2018-19 and current year 2019 -20 (upto Septem ber, 2019) is given at \nAnnexure-II. \n \n(b) :   The details of the allocation of power from Central Generating \nStations to each State/UT during the last three years i.e. 2016 -17 (as on \n31.03.2017), 2017-18 (as on 31.03.2018) and 2018 -19 (as on 31.03.2019), \nare given at Annexures III, IV and V respectively. \n\n \n(c) & (d) :  In case of need and request from the States/UTs, Government of \nIndia/Ministry of Power has been considering enhancement of the power \nallocation to the States/UTs from the unallocated quota of the Central \nGenerating Stations, keeping in view the overall availability and \nrequirement of the various States/UTs. \n \nDuring the year 2019 -20 (upto October 2019), requests for \nadditional allocation of power were  received from Puducherry, Andhra \nPradesh, Assam and Jammu & Kashmir, and request for reducing \nallocation was received from Chandigarh. The details in this regard are as \nunder: \n \n(i) In June, 2019, Puducherry had requested for  continuation of their \nallocation of 40 MW  from unallocated quota of Central Generating \nStations of Southern Region with effect from 01.07.2019 and the same was \nallowed accordingly. \n \n(ii) In August, 2019, Andhra Pradesh had requested for additional \nallocation of 1000 MW  of power for the month of September, 2019, and \nthe same was allocated from the  surrendered share of other States in \nCentral Generating Stations of Northern and Western Regions. \n \n(iii) Assam had in October, 2019, requested for allocation of 65 MW and \nit was recommended that the same can be considered for allocation from \nthe surrendered  share of other States in Central Generating Stations of \nNTPC if Assam gives its consent.  \n \n(iv) In October, 2019, Jammu & Kashmir requested for an enhancement \nin the allocation of power for the winter months. The allocation to Jammu \n& Kashmir from  the unallocated quota of Central Generating Stations in \nNorthern Region has been  increased from 30% to 78% of unallocated \npower of Northern region for the winter months. \n \n(v) Chandigarh had in October, 2019, requested for reduction in their \nallocation from 14%  to 9% from the unallocated quota of Central \nGenerating Stations in Northern Region  and the same has been given \neffect to.  \n \n********* \n \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n747 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 21.11.2019. \n*************** \nGeneration details of Central Generating Stations during the last three years and current year upto October, 2019.  \nNAME OF STATION Fuel *Monitored \nCapacity as \non \n31.10.2019 \nMW \nGENERATION (MU) \n 2016-17      2017-18   2018-19  *2019-20 \n(upto-Oct. \n19)    \nBADARPUR TPS COAL 0.00 1704.85 1556.97 1400.33 0.00 \nBARAUNI TPS COAL 710.00 - - 44.85 80.25 \nBARH II COAL 1320.00 7642.01 9272.35 9845.23 5749.11 \nBHILAI TPS COAL 500.00 3651.19 3877.77 3426.23 1742.18 \nBOKARO `B` TPS COAL 210.00 1456.68 573.94 688.44 94.03 \nBOKARO TPS `A` EXP COAL 500.00 208.00 2924.25 2920.75 1523.32 \nBONGAIGAON TPP COAL 750.00 1680.27 1732.17 2824.21 2197.67 \nCHANDRAPURA(DVC) TPS COAL 630.00 5642.70 4075.82 3560.59 1978.61 \nDADRI (NCTPP) COAL 1820.00 8765.46 9933.39 10533.54 4608.69 \nDURGAPUR STEEL TPS COAL 1000.00 6655.77 6503.85 6293.54 3613.01 \nDURGAPUR TPS COAL 210.00 592.72 947.10 981.28 218.33 \nFARAKKA STPS COAL 2100.00 13743.98 13356.91 14846.44 7534.09 \nGADARWARA TPP COAL 800.00 - - 0.00 623.06 \nINDIRA GANDHI STPP COAL 1500.00 5473.77 7734.53 7387.28 2260.28 \nKAHALGAON TPS COAL 2340.00 15947.93 16316.65 16485.91 9492.38 \nKHARGONE STPP COAL 660.00 - - - 198.89 \nKODARMA TPP COAL 1000.00 3801.77 5911.30 627"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |748", "t": "Allotment of DDA Flats", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n LOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.748 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON NOVEMBER 21, 2019  \nALLOTMENT OF DDA FLATS \nNo. 748 SHRIMATI MEENAKASHI LEKHI: \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be \npleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Delhi Development Authority (DDA) flats have not \nbeen a success among middle and lower income group \nfamilies and if so, the reasons therefor; \n(b) the steps taken to address the decline in sale of such flats; \nand \n(c) the steps taken to provide DDA flats for lower income group \nfamilies? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n(a): Delhi Development Authority (DDA) has informed that the total \nnumber of flats included in the Housing Scheme, 2019 were 17,922 \nand the number of applications received for these flats were \n45,045.  In total 8,438 flats were allotted out of which  6,070 flats \nhave been surrendered till date.  In majority of cases, the \napplicants have not provided any specific reasons for surrender.  \nHowever, location and s ize of the allotted flats are reported to be \nthe main reasons for surrender.  \n….2/- \n\n \n-2- \n \n(b): DDA has taken f ollowing steps to tackle the issue of decline \nin sale.  \n(i) Reduced the prices of Economically Weaker Section (EWS)  \ncategory flats.  \n(ii) Wide publicity through leading newspaper, FM radio and DDA \nwebsite. \n(iii) Scheme of amalgamation of Lower Income Group (LIG) flats at \nNarela Sector G7/G8. \n(c): DDA has launched a new Scheme for allotment of 6,273 EWS \ncategory flats at concessional r ates with effect from 30.08.2019. \nThis scheme will be open till 30.11.2019. \n**** \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |749", "t": "Smart Name Plates", "head": "O.I.H  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n   \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 749 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON NOVEMBER 21, 2019  \n \nSMART NAME PLATES \n \n \nNO. 749.     SHRI RAVI KISHAN: \n                   DR. RAM SHANKAR KATHERIA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to  \nstate : \n(a)   whether the Government is proposing to install smart name plates \n       in every house in the smart cities by which the property tax,  \n       electricity and water bills and even garbage collection can be  \n       tracked;  \n(b)  if so, the details along with the selected smart cities in the priority  \n      list where such arrangements will be made; and \n(c)  the details of the facilities likely to be linked to the smart name \n      plates? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (c) The objective of Smart Cities Mission (SCM) is to promote cities \nthat provide core infrastructure such as adequate wa ter supply, \nassured electricity supply, sanitation including solid waste \nmanagement, efficient urban mobility and public transport, etc. to  \n…2/- \n\n \n-2- \ngive a clean and sustainable environment and a decent quality of life \nto its citizens through application of `Smart’ solutions. The \nimplementation of the SCM at the City level is done by a Special \nPurpose Vehicle (SPV) created for the purpose. The SPV plan, \nappraise, approve, release funds, implement, manage, operate, \nmonitor and evaluate the Smart City development projects. A total of \n5,151 different projects, at an estimated cost of Rs. 2,05,018 crores, \nhave been included by the 100 selected Cities in their S mart City \nproposals. Some Smart Cities i.e. Ujjain, Jalandhar, Amritsar & \nBhopal have reported projects related to installation of Smart Name \nPlates which may be linked to collection of electricity bills, water \nbills, property tax, license fees etc in these Smart Cities.  \n…… "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |75", "t": "Employment in Informal Sector", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 75 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH NOVEMBER, 2019 \n \nEMPLOYMENT IN INFORMAL SECTOR \n \n75. MS. MALA ROY: \n  \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the people employed in the informal sector during the previous three financial \nyears; and   \n \n(b) the effect of demonetization on jobs in the informal sector? \n \n \nANSWER \n MINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT  \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) &  (b):  As per the results of Periodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS) conducted by \nNational Sample Survey Office, Ministry of Statistics and Programme \nImplementation during 2017-18, the percentage of workers engaged in proprietary \nand partnership enterprises i n usual status basis (principal status+subsidiary) in \nnon-agriculture and AGEGC sectors(The coverage of AGEGC Sector is \n[Ag]riculture sector [E]xcluding [G]rowing of [C]rops, market gardening, \nhorticulture and growing of crops combined with farming of anim als)was 68.4% in \nthe country which is a major part of informal sector. \nFurther, as per the results of Periodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS) and annual \nemployment-unemployment surveys conducted by Labour Bureau, Ministry of \nLabour and Employment, the estimate d Worker Population Ratio  on Usual status \n(principal status+subsidiary) basis for persons aged 15years and above  to the \nextent available is  given below: \nState PLFS  Survey by Labour Bureau  \n2017-18 2015-16 2013-14 \nAll India 46.8% 50.5% 53.7% \n(Note: Survey methodology and sample selection are different in PLFS and Labour \nBureau survey). \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |750", "t": "Schemes for Drinking Water", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.750  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2019  \n  \nSchemes for Drinking Water \n†750. SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of schemes for drinking water in the country;  \n(b) the expenditure incurred on the said schemes du ring the last three years, State-wise \nincluding Bundelkhand;  \n \n(c) the measures taken by the Government to make wa ter management and drinking \nwater supply more effective; and  \n \n(d) the number of persons for which drinking water is not available in the country, \nState-wise including Bundelkhand? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA) \n \n(a) National Rural Drinking Water Programme (NRDWP)  is a Centrally Sponsored \nscheme, under which financial and technical assista nce is provided to States/ UTs for \nrural water supply. This has now been restructured and subsumed intoJal Jeevan \nMission (JJM) which aims atenabling every rural hou sehold to have a Functional \nHousehold Tap Connection (FHTC) for providing potab le water at service level of 55 \nlitre per capita per day (lpcd) by 2024. \n \n(b) State-wise fund released under NRDWP including the States of Madhya Pradesh and \nUttar Pradesh covering Bundelkhand during the last three years is Annexed . \n \n(c) Government of India has launched Jal Shakti Abh iyan (JSA), a water conservation \ncampaign, which is being implemented in two phases in 256 water-stressed districts of \nthe country. The Phase–I was implemented from 1st July, 2019 to 30th September, 2019 \nacross the country and Phase–II is being implemente dfrom 1st October, 2019 to 30th \nNovember, 2019in the Southern States which receive retreating monsoon. During the \ncampaign, officers, groundwater experts and scienti sts from the Government of India \nhave worked with State and District officials in th ese districts to promote water \n\nconservation and water resource management by focus ing on accelerated \nimplementation of five target interventions, viz. w ater conservation & rainwater \nharvesting, renovation of traditional and other wat er bodies/ tanks, reuse and recharge \nof bore wells, watershed development and intensive afforestation. With this campaign, \nhuge awareness has been generated and various stake holders, viz. government \ndepartments, agencies, NGOs, officials, Panchayats,  individuals, etc. have started taking \nsteps for water conservation.  \n \nFor effective drinking water supply, provision has been made under JJM for \nproviding rural households with a service level of 55 lpcd against 40 lpcd norm under \nerstwhile NRDWP. \n(d) Under erstwhile National Rural Drinking Water P rogramme (NRDWP), the coverage \nwas monitored in terms of Fully Covered (FC) habita tions i.e. having provision of \nminimum 40 litre per capita per day (lpcd) of potab le drinking water with sources at a \nreasonable distance. As reported by States/ UTs, as  on 15.11.2019, out of total 17.24 \nlakh rural habitations, around 14 lakh habitationsw ith 76.58% rural population are \nfully covered with provision of 40 litre or more pe r capita per day (lpcd) and 2.67 \nlakhhabitations with19.67% rural population are partially covered, i.e. with provision of \nless than 40 lpcd whereas 55,721 habitations with 3 .75% rural population are having \nsources with quality issues. \n***** \n  \n\nAnnex referred in the reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 750 due for \nreply on 21/11/2019 \nState-wise fund released under NRDWP including the States of Madhya Pradesh \nand Uttar Pradesh covering Bundelkhand during the last three years \nAmount in \u0001 Crore \n \nS. No. State/ UT 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 \n1. Andaman & Nicobar Islands 0  0 0.31  \n2. Andhra Pradesh 157.38  233.09  185.85  \n3. Arunachal Pradesh 95.71  63.08  90.89  \n4. Assam 203.09  474.8  300.76  \n5. Bihar 470.96  355.6  234.84  \n6. Chhattisgarh 65.66  49.84  48.19  \n7. Goa 3.35  0 1.67  \n8. Gujarat 265.16  290.86  222.27  \n9. Haryana 114.2  88.82  76.76  \n10. Himachal Pradesh 64.73  101.85  85.43  \n11. Jammu & Kashmir (Including \nLadakh) 219.94  325.33  249.34  \n12. Jharkhand 157.89  209.09  85.12  \n13. Karnataka 339.83  365.81  276.06  \n14. Kerala 74.21  95.16  84.86  \n15. Madhya Pradesh 212.48  135.51  243.62  \n16. Maharashtra 412.32  282.47  239.06  \n17. Manipur 18.87  66.25  37.73  \n18. Meghalaya 50  85.91  49.15  \n19. Mizoram 24.82  46.1  26.25  \n20. Nagaland 36.2  17.88  17.36  \n21. Odisha 100.14  83.59  128.82  \n22. Punjab 53.56  173.29  119.41  \n23. Rajasthan 681.21  714.24  655.41  \n24. Sikkim 15.21  17.51  10.89  \n25. Tamil Nadu 175.08  185.1  167.31  \n26. Telangana 111.89  843.56  123.18  \n27. Tripura 38.73  83.93  51.73  \n28. Uttar Pradesh 639.54  472.52  670.72  \n29. Uttarakhand 99.69  110.28  92.97  \n30. West Bengal 423.68  996.68  890.28  \n Total 5,325.53  6,968.15  5,466.24  \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |752", "t": "Financial Assistance to UP under PMAY", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 752 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON NOVEMBER 21, 2019 \n \nFINANCIAL ASSISTANCE TO UP UNDER PMAY  \n \n \nNO. 752. SHRI SATYADEV PACHAURI: \n \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to  \nstate: \n \n(a)   whether the Government has any records of financial assistance \nprovided to Uttar Pradesh under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana  \n(PMAY) during the last three years; and  \n \n(b)    if so, the details thereof, District and year-wise and if not, the reasons \nthereof?  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n \n \n \n(a) &(b):Yes, Sir. Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (MoHUA) is \nimplementing Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana (Urban) [PMAY(U)] since \n25.06.2015 for providing assistance to States/Union Territories (UTs) \nincluding the State of Uttar Pradesh, for addressing the housing \nrequirement in urban areas. \n…2/- \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n-2- \n \n \nA total of 14,42,975 houses have been approved for the State of Uttar \nPradesh; out of this, 8,33,823 are at various stages of construction and \n3,19,486 have been completed/ delivered. Central Assistance of Rs \n21,992.42 crore has been approved; out of this, Rs. 7,544.96 crore have so \nfar been released to the State. \n \nDuring the last three years , Central Assistance of Rs.5540.47 Crore has \nbeen released to the State of Uttar Pradesh. Year -wise and District -wise \ndetails of Central Assistance released to the State are at Annexure. \n****** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to LSUQ No - 752 due for 21.11.2019 \nDistrict-wise and year-wise details for Central Assistance released during last \nthree year