nThiruvananthapura\nm 6 189.11 7 69.53 2 136.79 15 395.43 \nLakshadweep"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |86", "t": "Agreement with other Countries", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA  \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 86 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.11.2019 \n \nAGREEMENT WITH OTHER COUNTRIES  \n \n†86. SHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA: \n  \n Will the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPR ENEURSHIP be \npleased to state : \n \n(a)  whether the Government has signed any agreemen t with other countries to \ndevelop vocational skills in various sectors; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the specialization sectors for which agreement has been signed along with the \nname of the countries? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE \n MINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n \n(a) to (c) Ministry of Skill Development and Entrep reneurship has signed Fifteen (15) \nMemoranda of Understanding/Agreements/Joint Declara tion of Intent with Nine (9) \ncountries in the area of skill development and Vocational Education & Training, enhance \nand sharing of lessons learned in skill development, professional education and training, \nmutual recognition of qualifications to facilitate mobility of skilled work force, capacity \nbuilding and development of competence based curric ula etc. A Statement indicating \nthe name of countries with which this Ministry has signed various Memoranda of \nUnderstanding/Agreements/Joint Declaration of Inten t along with areas of collaboration \nis at Annexure . \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE  \n \nAnnexure as referred to in reply to parts (a) to (c ) of  Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo. 86 for answer on 18.11.2019 asked by Sh. Vishnu  Datt Sharma regarding \n“Agreement with other Countries”. \n \nS.No \n. \nName of \nthe \nCountry \nType of Agreement  Sectors covered  \n1. \n \nGermany \n \nJoint Memorandum of \nUnderstanding. \nCooperation in the field of Skill Development and \nVET, Dual apprenticeships and workplace-based \nskill development, Capacity Building including \nCompetency based curricula, Training of VET \ncluster managers etc. \nImplementation Agreement. Public and private sector s work together to \ncreate conditions conducive to improving \ncooperative vocational training in India. \nJoint Declaration of Intent  Training of VET Cluste r Managers and Indian \nSkill Development Officers that are directly \nengaged in the design and implementation of the \nprocesses of Dual VET in Clusters \n  Joint Declaration of Intent \non Skills for sustainable \ngrowth  \nVocational Education and Training \nJoint Declaration of Intent. \n \nSkill for sustainable growth  \n2   Japan  \n \nMemorandum of \nCooperation. \nTo train future shop floor leaders of Japanese \nstandard level in India with Japanese method of \nworking and to establish Japanese Endowed \nCourse (JEC) with existing engineering colleges \nin India to train future candidates for middle \nmanagement engineers in manufacturing sector. \nMemorandum of \nCooperation  \nTechnical Intern Training Programme (TITP) \n3 Switzerla \nnd  \nMemorandum of \nUnderstanding. \nSkills development, professional education and \ntraining. Sharing good practices in the areas of \nskill development. Capacity building of \nStakeholders through facilitating networks and \npartnership. \n4 United \nArabia \nEmirates \nMemorandum of \nUnderstanding  \nEnhance cooperation on Skill development and \nmutual recognition of cooperation to facilitate \nmobility of skilled workers from India to UAE.  \n\n5 Qatar Memorandum of \nUnderstanding  \nRecognition of professional and Trade \nQualifications in both countries. Recognition of \nexpertise of workers pre and post to the \nrecruitment process.  \n6 China Memorandum of \nunderstanding. \nTo strengthen technical cooperation through \nsharing experiences, best practices and \nknowledge in the field of skill Development.  \n7 Belarus Memorandum of \nUnderstanding  \nCurricula design, training and evaluation \nmethodologies, content, development, different \nlearning arrangements. \n8 UK \n \nImplementation Agreement  Promoting and expanding b ilateral relations in \nthe field of skill development.  \n \nMemorandum of \nUnderstanding  \nStrengthening skill delivery in high demand \nsectors where UK has technical and skilling \nexpertise, capacity building of institutions, \ntechnical assistance for a apprenticeships, \nquality assessment and certification. \n9 France  Memorandum of \nunderstanding  \nDevelopment of competence- based curricula \nand their dissemination within the training \nsystem. to build up capacities in training institutes \nand within micro, small and medium enterprises.  \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |860", "t": "Atal Jyoti Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 860 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11. 2019 \n \nATAL JYOTI YOJANA \n \n860. SHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE  \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) the targets set under the Atal Jyoti Yojana for installation of street lights in the country \nincluding Maharashtra; and  \n \n(b) the status of its implementation and achievements made thereunder especially in Maharashtra \nas on date, State/UT-wise? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (I/C) FOR NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY,  POWER and MoS \nfor  SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R. K. SINGH) \n(a) Under Phase-I of Atal Jyoti Yojana (AJAY), the target was to install around 2000 solar street \nlights (SSLs) in each of the 169 parliamentary constituencies in the states of Assam, Bihar, \nJharkhand, Odisha and Uttar Pradesh by 31 March 2018. \n \nUnder Phase-II of Atal Jyoti Yojana  the target is to install 3.04 lakh SSLs in the following \nstates/regions:  \ni. States of Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Jharkhand, Odisha and Assam, which were covered in \nPhase-I of the Scheme.  \nii. Hilly states of Jammu & Kashmir, Himachal Pradesh and Uttarakhand. \niii. North Eastern states including Sikkim. \niv. Island UTs Andaman & Nicobar and Lakshadweep.  \nv. Parliamentary Constituencies covering 48 Aspirational Districts of states other than those \ncovered in (i) to (iii) above.  \nIn four Aspirationa l Districts namely Nandurbar, Gadchiroli, Washim and Osmanabad of \nMaharashtra MNRE plans to install 5,500 SSLs under Phase-II of Atal Jyoti Yojana.  \n \n(b) Under Phase-I of Atal Jyoti Yojana  1,34,342 of solar street lights have been installed in the \nstates of Assam, Bihar, Jharkhand, Odisha and Uttar Pradesh as on 31.10.2019.  \n \nUnder Phase -II of Atal Jyoti Yojana 13,583 solar street lights have been installed as on \n31.10.2019.  \nState-wise break-up of installations under Phase-I and Phase-II Schemes is placed at Annexure.  \nFor aspirational districts of Maharashtra covered under AJAY Phase -II, sanction is yet to be \nreceived for 25% of the cost of installation of Solar street lights in the concerned  Parliamentary \nConstituencies from respective MPLADS Fund.  \n \n*****  \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure- referred to in Reply to part (b) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 860 for \n21/11/2019 regarding Atal Jyoti Yojana  \nDETAILS OF SOLAR STREET LIGHTS INSTALLED UNDER ATAL JYOTI YOJANA \nPHASE-I AND PHASE-II SCHEMES \n \nA. Solar street lights installed under Atal Jyoti Yojana (AJAY): Phase-I \n \n \nS.No. State  Total installation done till 31st \nOctober 2019 (Nos.) \n1 UP 78,722 \n2 Bihar 29693 \n3 Jharkhand 10,535 \n4 Odisha 8,733 \n5 Assam 6,659 \n TOTAL 1,34,342 \n \n \nB. Solar street lights installed under Atal Jyoti Yojana (AJAY): \nPhase-II \n \nS. No State  Total installation done till 31st \nOctober, 2019 (Nos.) \n1 Uttar Pradesh 2,449 \n2 Bihar 3,812 \n3 Jharkhand 1,442 \n4 Odisha 2,028 \n5 Madhya Pradesh 663 \n6 Rajasthan 262 \n7 Uttarakhand 2,740 \n8 Gujarat 187 \nTotal 13,583 \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |862", "t": "Work on NH-2 in Nagaland", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 862 \nANSWERED ON 21ST NOVEMBER, 2019 \n \nWORK ON NH-2 IN NAGALAND \n862.    SHRI TOKHEHO YEPTHOMI:   \n \n             Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n                      सड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the details and the present status of two -lane highway construction work on NH -2 in \nNagaland; and  \n \n(b)  the time by which the said work is likely to be completed? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) & (b) Construction of 2-laning in 48.18 km of NH 2 is in progress. Details of ongoing projects are \nas under: \nSr. \nNo. \nName of project Length \n(km.) \nSanction \nCost \n(Rs. in Cr.) \nTarget \ndate of \ncompletion  \n1. Widening to two lane with paved shoulder & \nStrengthening from Km. 40.000 to Km. 56.000 \nof NH-61(New NH -2). \n16.00 167.62 07.12.2020 \n2. Widening to two lane with paved shoulder &  \nStrengthening from Km. 56.000 to Km. 72.400 \nof NH-61(New NH -2). \n16.40 172.63 07.12.2020 \n3. Widening to two lane with paved shoulder & \nStrengthening from Km. 168.000 to Km. \n183.783 of NH-61(New NH -2). \n15.78 217.35 15.11.2020 \n Total 48.18 557.6  \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |863", "t": "Utilising Land Banks of PSEs for Housing", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 863 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON NOVEMBER 21, 2019 \nUTILISING LAND BANKS OF PSEs FOR HOUSING \n \nNO. 863. SHRI NATARAJAN P.R.: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken a decision to use land \nparcels belonging to sick and closing Public Sector Enterprises \n(PSEs) for the construction of housing schemes including BSNL \nland bank; \n \n(b) if so, the details of such land parcels identified so far and their \nvalue as on date with the details of names of PSUs and cities \nwhere these are available; \n \n(c) the details of funding mode decided for investment in these \nhousing schemes; and \n \n(d) if not, the timeline by which is will be finalised? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (d) At present, no such decision has been taken by the \nGovernment. \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |864", "t": "Drinking Water Supply in Drought Affected Areas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 864  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2019  \n  \nDrinking Water Supply in Drought Affected Areas \n†864.SHRI NIHAL CHAND: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) the steps taken by the Government to provide cl ean drinking water in drought \naffected areas of the country including Rajasthan;  \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to provide spec ial assistance to solve the \nproblem related to drinking water in rural areas of the country;  \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(d) the details of the assistance provided to diffe rent States of the country including \nRajasthan during the last two years under National Rural Drinking Water Programme \nand the effective steps taken to solve the problem of drinking water? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA) \n \n(a) & (b) To enable every rural household in all St ates/ UTs including Rajasthan to have \nFunctional Household Tap Connection (FHTC) for prov iding potable water at the rate of \n55 litre per capita per day (lpcd) by 2024, Governm ent of India has launched Jal Jeevan \nMission (JJM), with an outlay of \u0001 3.60 lakh Crore . Under JJM, funds are provided to \nStates/ UTs based on approved criteria which, interalia  includes weightage to area \nunder erstwhile Desert Development Programme (DDP) and Drought Prone Area \nProgramme (DPAP) as well as number of households wi thout tap connection. During \ncurrent year, Rajasthan has been allocated \u0001856.47 Crore out of which \u0001428.24 Crore \nhas been released.   \n(c) Does not arise. \n(d) Under erstwhile National Rural Drinking Water P rogramme (NRDWP), now \nsubsumed into JJM, for providing rural water supply , Government of India provided \nfinancial and technical assistance to States/ UTs i ncluding Rajasthan. The details of the \nfinancial assistance provided to different States o f the country including Rajasthan \nduring the last two years under NRDWP are annexed. Further to the launch of JJM, a \nconference of Minister in-charge of rural water supply of various States was held in New \nDelhi followed by five regional workshops to discus s various aspects of JJM and \nmodalities of the implementation. In addition, regu lar review of the programme is also \nheld States/ UTs. \n****  \n\nAnnex referred in the reply to LokSabhaUnstarred Question No. 864 due for reply \non 21/11/2019 \nDetails of financial assistanceprovided to States i ncluding Rajasthan during the \nlast two years under National Rural Drinking Water Programme (NRDWP) \n(in \u0001 Crore)  \nS. No. State/ UT 2017-18 2018-19 \n1.  Andaman & Nicobar Islands 0 0.31 \n2.  Andhra Pradesh 233.09 185.85 \n3.  Arunachal Pradesh 63.08 90.89 \n4.  Assam 474.8 300.76 \n5.  Bihar 355.6 234.84 \n6.  Chhattisgarh 49.84 48.19 \n7.  Goa 0 1.67 \n8.  Gujarat 290.86 222.27 \n9.  Haryana 88.82 76.76 \n10.  Himachal Pradesh 101.85 85.43 \n11.  Jammu & Kashmir (Including \nLadakh) 325.33 249.34 \n12.  Jharkhand 209.09 85.12 \n13.  Karnataka 365.81 276.06 \n14.  Kerala 95.16 84.86 \n15.  Madhya Pradesh 135.51 243.62 \n16.  Maharashtra 282.47 239.06 \n17.  Manipur 66.25 37.73 \n18.  Meghalaya 85.91 49.15 \n19.  Mizoram 46.1 26.25 \n20.  Nagaland 17.88 17.36 \n21.  Odisha 83.59 128.82 \n22.  Punjab 173.29 119.41 \n23.  Rajasthan 714.24 655.41 \n24.  Sikkim 17.51 10.89 \n25.  Tamil Nadu 185.1 167.31 \n26.  Telangana 843.56 123.18 \n27.  Tripura 83.93 51.73 \n28.  Uttar Pradesh 472.52 670.72 \n29.  Uttarakhand 110.28 92.97 \n30.  West Bengal 996.68 890.28 \n Total 6,968.15 5,466.24 \n         [Source: IMIS, DDWS]  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |865", "t": "Programmes/Schemes for Development of Sports", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  865 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2019 \n \nProgrammes/Schemes for Development of Sports \n \n865.    DR. T.R.PAARIVENDHAR: \nSHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is implementing any special programmes/ \nschemes for the development of sports in the country and if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government has reviewed any programmes announced \nearlier, if so, the details thereof and the current status of Khelo India \nProgramme; \n \n(c) the steps taken by the  Government for preparation for Tokyo \nOlympics, 2020; \n \n(d) the status of India’s preparation for the 2020 Tokyo Olympics; \n \n(e) whether the Government is tracking the progress of the players and \ntaking care of their financial and dietary needs and if so, the details thereof; \nand \n \n(f) whether there is no table tennis coach for Indian players since 2018 \nAsian Games and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n{ SHRI KIREN RIJIJU } \n \n(a) Yes Sir. The ‘Khelo India – National Programme for Development of \nSports’ was launched in October, 2017 with the aim to strengthen the entire  \nsports ecosystem to promote the twin objectives of mass participation and \n\npromotion of excellence in sports across the country. All the verticals of \nKhelo India Scheme focus on broad -basing of sports amongst the youth of \nthe country. The 12 verticals under Khelo India Scheme are:  \n \ni. Play Field Development \nii. Utilization and Creation/ Upgradation of Sports Infrastructure \niii. Sports for Peace and Development \niv. State Level Khelo India Centers \nv. Annual Sports Competitions  \nvi. Talent Search and Development   \nvii. Sports for Women \nviii. Support to National/Regional/State Sports Academies  \nix. Promotion of Sports among persons with disabilities \nx. Promotion of rural and indigenous/tribal games \nxi. Physical Fitness of School going Children \nxii. Community Coaching Development \n \n(b) Khelo India Scheme was approved for three years i.e., from 2016-17 to \n2019-20. Review of Khelo India scheme is scheduled on completion of the \ntenure of the scheme. \n \n(c) to (e) The Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports (MYAS)  is providing \ncustomised support to Olympic prospects athletes under the  Target \nOlympic Podium Scheme (TOPS)  for the 2020 Olympic Games and \nParalympic Games. Monthly stipend of Rs. 50,000/ - to each athlete is also \nprovided under TOPS. TOPS is currently supporting 87 athletes across 12 \nsports disciplines. \n \nA t otal of 62 athletes (30 Individual athletes + 16 member Men’s \nHockey Team +16 member Women’s Hockey Team) have already qualified \nfor the TOKYO 2020 Olympics.  In addition 23 Para -athletes have qualified \nfor the TOKYO 2020 Paralympics. \n \nUnder the scheme of ‘Assistance to National Sports Federation s’, \nidentified promising sportspersons/teams are provided with all the requisite \nfacilities through respective National Sports Federation for their \npreparations, such as wholesome nutritious diet, food supplements, \nequipments, state -of-the-art infrastructure, lodging & travelling facilities, \nservices of reputed Indian and Foreign Coaches/Supporting staff, Scientific \n& Medical supports, Sports Kit etc. besides financial assistance for their \nabroad training and partici pation in International Competitions in India and \nabroad.  \n\n \n(f) The Sports Authority of India (SAI) has 19 Coaches, including 2 \nForeign Coaches, for Table Tennis. \n \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |866", "t": "Progress in respect of Small Cities", "head": "O.I.H. \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n   \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 866 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON NOVEMBER 21, 2019 \n \nPROGRESS IN RESPECT OF SMALL CITIES \n \nNO. 866.     SHRI DULAL CHAND GOSWAMI: \n \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate : \n \n(a)  the details of the progress made in development works in cities \nidentified to be developed as smart cities, State-wise;  \n \n(b)  whether the Government is demolishing the slums under this \nproject;  \n(c)   if so, the number of slums demolished all over the country, State -\nwise, including State of Bihar;  \n(d)  the steps taken by the Government for the rehabilitation of the \ndisplaced families as a result of demolishing of the slums; and \n (e) the funds allocated for the rehabilitation and the money spent \nthereon, State-wise? \n \n \n \n \n…2/- \n \n\nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) : State/UT-wise details  of progress made under Smart Cities \nMission are attached in Annexure-I. \n(b) to (d): Land  and Colonisation being State subjects, State \nGovernments issue policies/guidelines or funds for slum re -\ndevelopment. No specific guid elines or funds for \ndemolition/rehabilitation of the people living in slums have been \nissued under the Smart Cities Mission (SCM). Under SCM, all \nprojects are proposed and implemented by the cities  in \naccordance with the Mission Statement and Guidelines. Wi th \nregards to Slum development projects,  27 cities out of the \nselected 100  Smart Cities  have reported a total of 45 slum re -\ndevelopment projects at an estimated cost of Rs 3,967 crore. \nSome projects require re -settlement of slum dwellers . Their \ndetails, as per information provided by the ci ties, are given in \nAnnexure-II. \n \n(e):  No separate funds are allocated by the Central Government \nunder SCM to the States/UTs for rehabilitation purposes, however, \ncities may allocate a part of the project cost  for the purpose. \nFurther, under Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana, one of the verticals is \nrelated to slum re-development. When such projects are included , \nas a part of Smart City Proposal under convergence , benefits \nprovided under PMAY-U are utilized by the concerned Smart Cities.  \n*****  \n\nAnnexure-I to Part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.866 \nfor 21.11.2019 by Shri Dulal Chand Goswami regarding Progress  in respect of Small Cities \n                                                                                                                                                      Amount (in Rs Crore) \nState/UT \nTender Issued \n Work Order Issued Work Completed \n Total \nNo of \nProjects Amount No of \nProjects Amount No of \nProjects Amount No of \nProjects Amount \nAndaman Nicobar \nIslands 3 17.35 1 6.00 2 14.33 6 37.68 \nAndhra Pradesh 23 890.83 133 6810.66 60 793.78 216 8495.27 \nArunachal Pradesh 0 0.00 2 171.89 0 0.00 2 171.89 \nAssam 4 522.51 5 33.93 2 5.00 11 561.44 \nBihar 30 1730.28 36 2167.53 8 527.88 74 4425.69 \nChandigarh 14 1120.05 14 128.37 17 196.01 45 1444.43 \nChhattisgarh 34 846.46 37 1242.25 72 488.18 143 2576.89 \nDadra and Nagar \nHaveli 0 0.00 0 0.00 0 0.00 0 0.00 \nDaman and Diu 4 491.91 3 53.18 1 0.29 8 545.38 \nDelhi 7 155.00 10 698.94 80 427.93 97 1281.87 \nGoa 4 43.68 9 333.57 9 14.77 22 392.02 \nGujarat 69 4790.58 129 9066.18 131 3464.70 329 17321.46 \nHaryana 14 569.88 25 596.53 4 162.11 43 1328.52 \nHimachal Pradesh 5 229.26 23 237.84 12 207.37 40 674.47 \nJammu and \nKashmir 7 378.34 13 258.97 0 0.00 20 637.31 \nJharkhand 1 586.46 26 2553.87 2 49.57 29 3189.90 \nKarnataka 117 3453.77 344 9875.90 193 1259.71 654 14589.38 \nKerala 20 831.69 50 834.75 5 166.57 75 1833.01 \nLakshadweep 1 100.00 2 6.80 2 1.15 5 107.95 \nMadhya Pradesh 72 6571.29 225 10987.95 265 5275.89 562 22835.13 \nMaharashtra 51 9495.81 119 7011.40 77 2195.20 247 18702.41 \nManipur 13 170.75 4 275.16 0 0.00 17 445.91 \nMeghalaya 1 50.00 1 0.02 0 0.00 2 50.02 \nMizoram 1 1.50 15 290.99 5 37.12 21 329.61 \nNagaland 3 62.08 8 259.07 6 119.70 17 440.85 \nOdisha 8 1906.59 23 2465.70 13 838.48 44 5210.77 \nPuducherry 6 14.44 7 49.83 1 12.60 14 76.87 \nPunjab 18 975.45 26 898.53 3 9.16 47 1883.14 \nRajasthan 92 1097.80 127 3862.55 87 920.38 306 5880.73 \nSikkim 6 357.25 19 815.06 1 7.64 26 1179.95 \nTamil Nadu 38 1740.80 236 10823.83 72 563.49 346 13128.12 \nTelangana 12 457.58 39 692.60 9 515.46 60 1665.64 \nTripura 13 203.81 28 509.92 14 13.73 55 727.46 \nUttar Pradesh 74 3658.15 289 7188.32 136 4834.63 499 15681.10 \nUttarakhand 11 343.17 22 997.84 3 46.35 36 1387.36 \nWest Bengal 26 189.39 30 82.16 4 1.10 60 272.65 \nGrand Total 802 44053.91 2080 82288.09 1296 23170.28 4178 149512.28 \n \n \n\nAnnexure-II to Part (b) to (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.866 \nfor 21.11.2019 by Shri Dulal Chand Goswami regarding Progress  in respect of Small Cities  \nState City Project \nAmount \n(in Rs \nCrore) Milestone \nAndhra \nPradesh Kakinada \nSmart community "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |867", "t": "Use of Nitrogen in Tyre", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 867 \nANSWERED ON 21ST NOVEMBER, 2019 \n \nUSE OF NITROGEN IN TYRE \n \n867.  MS. MALA ROY: \n \n Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes  to make use of Nitrogen in tyres mandatory  in the \ncountry; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) the measures taken/being taken by  the Government to control accidental  deaths on \nhighways especially the Yamuna Expressway? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) and (b) Filling of Nitrogen in tyres is available as an option to citizens at certain locations. \n \n(c)  Ministry of Road Transport and Highways is mandated with the task of constructing and \nmaintaining National Highways in the Country. Noida -Agra Yamuna Expressway is not a \nNational Highway constructed and managed by NHAI or this Ministry.  \n \n With regard to R oad Safety, the National Road Safety Policy outlines various policy \nmeasures such as promoting awareness, establishing road safety information data base, \nencouraging safer road infrastructure including application of intelligent transport, \nenforcement of safety laws. The Ministry has formulated a multi -pronged strategy to address \nthe issue of road safety based on Education, Engineering (both of roads and vehicles), \nEnforcement and Emergency Care. Further, the Ministry of Road Transport & Highways has \nconstituted a District Road Safety Committee in each district of the country to promote \nawareness amongst road users under the chairmanship of Hon'ble Member of Parliament \n(Lok Sabha) from the district. In addition to the above, the initiatives of the Ministry t o \npromote road safety are listed as below:  \n \n(i) Advocacy/Publicity campaign on road safety through the electronic media and print media \nto create awareness.  \n(ii) Issue of Guidelines for protection of Good Samaritans.  \n(iii) Setting up of model driving training Institutes in States.  \n\n(iv) Sanction of 22 Inspection and certification Centres for testing the fitness of the \ncommercial vehicles through an automated system  \n(v) Launch of mobile app for highway users i.e. \"Sukh ad Yatra 1033\" which enables \nhighways users to report potholes and other safety hazards on National Highways including \naccidents.  \n(vi) Observance of Road Safety Week every calendar year for spreading awareness and \nstrengthening road safety. \n(vii) Road safety has been made an integral part of road design at planning stage. \n(viii) The threshold for four laning of national highway has been reduced from 15,000 \nPassenger Car Units (PCUs) to 10,000 PCUs.  \n(ix) Safety standards for automobiles have been improved. \n(x) High priority has been accorded to identification and rectification of black spots (accident \nprone spots) on national highways.  \n(xi) Ministry has delegated powers to Regional Officers of MORTH for technical approval to \nthe detailed estimates for rect ification of identified Road Accident black spots for expediting \nthe rectification process to ensure safety of road users.  \n(xii) Guidelines for pedestrian facilities on National Highways for  persons with disabilities \nhave also been issued to all States / UTs.  \n(xiii) A Certification Course for Road  Safety Auditors has been co mmenced in India \nAcademy of Highway Engineers (IAHE) and 42 Auditors are certified. \n(xiv) Free Eye Check -up camp and distribution of eye glasses is conducted for truck/bus \ndrivers operating on NH. \n(xv) Removal of Liquor Shops as per directions of Hon’ble Supreme Court vide circular of \nF.No. RW/NH-33044/309/2016/S7R dated 06-04-2017 and 01-06-2017. \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |868", "t": "Impact of New Motor Vehicles Act", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 868 \nANSWERED ON 21ST NOVEMBER, 2019 \n \nIMPACT OF NEW MOTOR VEHICLES ACT  \n \n868.  SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total number of traffic fines levied after implementation of the Motor Vehicles \n(Amendment) Act, 2019 as on date; \n(b) the total amount collected as a result thereof, State/UT-wise; \n(c) the total number of road accidents and consequent deaths during the said period; \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to amend any of the provisions of the said Act in\n the near future; and \n(e)   if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) and (b)  As per the information available in the database of NIC (VAHAN, SARATHI) , the \ndetails of traffic fines levied after implementation of certain provisions of Motor Vehicles \n(Amendment) Act, 2019 and the total amount collected is as under : - \n \nSr. \nNo \nState No. of Challans Challan Booked for (Amount in Rs.) \n(Challans are being referred to courts. Actual \nRevenue is not available) \n1  Chandigarh        56,171 5,58,25,650.00 \n2  Pondicherry 219 25,500.00 \n3  Assam        334 15,45,500.00 \n4  Chhattisgarh     5,839 14,81,800.00 \n5  Uttar Pradesh          9,83,326 2,01,91,31,192.00 \n6  Odisha 1,02,098 37,78,88,071.00 \n7  Delhi          4,81,378 34,15,07,607.00 \n8  Rajasthan          38,102 5,56,77,662.00 \n9  Bihar 2,51,500 78,37,18,969.00 \n10  Dadra Nagar Haveli       2,013 6,28,400.00 \n11  Punjab          24,401 85,64,701.00 \n12  Goa     58 7,773.00 \n13  Uttarakhand         9,119 3,05,36,284.00 \n14  Tamil Nadu 14,13,996 27,75,61,250.00 \n15  Gujarat          2,22,945 1,01,27,85,400.00 \n16  Maharashtra    6,416 4,16,63,977.00 \n17  Himachal Pradesh 1,41,529 4,47,45,551.00 \n18  Haryana 99,962 72,18,84,608.00 \nTotal 38,39,406 5,77,51,79,895.00 \n\n(c)  As per information available with the Ministry  for the following State/UTs , the total \nnumber of road accidents consequent death is as under : - \n \nSl. \nNo. State Road Accidents Fatalities Percentage Change \n(+/-) Sept/Oct. 2018 Sept/Oct. 2019 \n1 Kerala 321 314 -2.1% \n2 Uttar Pradesh 1503 1355 -9.8% \n3 Bihar 459 411 -10.5% \n4 Gujarat 557 480 -13.8% \n5 Uttarakhand 78 61 -21.