truction Corporation (NBCC)  \n owes large sums of money to its vendors; \n(b)  If so, the quantum of sum/payment pending, vendor-wise; and \n(c)  the details and reasons of such pendency? \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (c) NBCC has informed that as on 10.11.2019, approximately \nRs. 1, 668 crore (excluding Arbitration cases) is pendin g for payment \ntowards vendors. Vendor-wise details are  available at web address  \nhttps://www.nbccindia.com/pdfData/Vendor/Appendix_I_and%20__II.pdf.\nThis pendency is mainly attributable to delay in receipt of funds fr om \nclients because of various reasons such as  pending approval for \ndeviations, extra item s, substitute items, escalation and extension of \ntime from clients, non -submission of documents as per terms and \nconditions of contract by the contractors etc. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |916", "t": "Four-Laning of Highways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 916 \nANSWERED ON 21ST NOVEMBER, 2019 \nFOUR-LANING OF HIGHWAYS \n916.  SHRI ASHOK KUMAR RAWAT:  \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n                                                 सड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken any step towards four -laning of Kanpur-Bilhaur-Kannauj \n–Etah-Bewar-Aligarh-Bulandshahr-Ghaziabad –Delhi G.T. road; \n(b) if so, the details and the present status thereof; and \n(c) the funds spent on the maintenance and repair of the said stretch during each of the last three \nyears? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) & (b) Yes Sir. The details of the project along with their present status are as below: \nSr. \nNo. \nProject Name Length Progress (in %) Remarks \n1 4/6 laning of Aligarh -\nBhadwas Section of NH -91 \n(Pkg.-I) \n45.83 Km \n \n3.99 Under \nimplementation \n2 4/6 laning of Aligarh -\nBhadwas Section of NH -91 \n(Pkg.-II) \n45.164 Km \n \n23.5 -do- \n3 4/6 laning of Aligarh-\nBhadwas Section of NH-91 \n(Pkg.-III) \n61.211 Km \n \n17.3 -do- \n4 4/6 laning of Naviganj – \nMitrasen section of NH -\n91(Pkg.-IV) \n67.00  Km NA At Tender Stage \n5 4/6 laning of Mitrasen  – \nKanpur section of NH -\n91 (Pkg.-V) \n58.00 Km NA At Tender Stage \n6 4laning of Ghaziabad -\nAligarh Section of NH-91 \n126.30 Km 99.00 Substantially \ncompleted, \n\n(c)  Ghaziabad –Aligarh section of National Highway (NH) has been developed on Design, \nBuild, Finance, Operate and Transfer (DBFOT) mode and maintenance of the stretch is the \nresponsibility of Concessionaire at his own cost. However, funds spent on maintenance and \nrepair of Aligarh –Kanpur Section of NH  during 2017 -18, 2018-19 and 2019-20 is Rs. 1.73 \ncrore, Rs. 8.28 crore and Rs. Nil respectively.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |917", "t": "Augmentation of Ticketing System by DMRC", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \n \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 917 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON NOVEMBER 21, 2019 \nAUGMENTATION OF TICKETING SYSTEM BY DMRC \nNO. 917. SHRI KOTHA PRABHAKAR REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased  \nto state: \n(a) whether the Delhi Metro Rail Corporation (DMRC) pr oposes to \naugment the ticketing system of stations falling under Phase I \nand II with special emphasis on unattended ticket sales and if \nso, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  whether a total of 240 additional access gates and 300 ticket \nvending machines proposed to be installed under the project \nand if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether automatic vending machines dedicated for card top \nup and installation of the access gates are al so scheduled to \nbe put up and if so, the details thereof alongwith the name of \nthe firm allocated the contract for the project; \n \n(d)  whether the DMRC is planning to focus on TVMs under the \nupcoming phase III and instead of setting up ticket counters, it \nwill have as many as 506 AVMs and if so, the details thereof; \nand \n \n(e)  whether most of the countries rely completely on automated \nmachines in metro journeys increasing the reliability on \nautomatic ticket generation in the new metro stations and if \nso, the details thereof and the present status in this regard \nalong with expenditure incurred so far?  \n…2/- \n \n \n\n-2- \n  \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n \n(a): Delhi Metro Rail Corporation Ltd. ( DMRC) has informed that   \naugmentation of the ticketing system of 105 stations out of 145 \nstations falling under Phase I and II has been done by installing 300 \nticket vending machines (TVMs) with feature of unattended ticket \nsales. Tender for supply and installation of additional 91  TVMs with \ncard top-up facility in remaining 40 stations of phase I & II has been \nfloated in October, 2019.  \n \n(b): 239 additional access gates in 41 stations and 300 additional \nTVMs have been installed in 105 stations of Phase I & II.  \n \n \n(c): DMRC has informed that 506 add value machines (AVMs)  \nsupplied by M/s Thales Communications and Security S .A.S, France \nwith dedicated card top-up facility have been installed  in 108 \nstations of phase III network . Contract for supply of 322 additional \naccess gates to be put up at various stations of phase I & II  has \nbeen awarded to M/s Revenue Collection System France SAS and \ncontract for installation of these gates  has been awarded to M/s \nAdept Telecom Pvt. Ltd., New Delhi. \n \n(d): DMRC has informed  that 591 TVMs have been installed in 108 \nstations of phase III network to improve passenger travel facilities \nand comfort. In additions, 506 add value machines (AVMs) have also \nbeen installed. \n \n(e): Such details are not available. \n \n***** \n\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |918", "t": "Identification of Linguistic Minorities", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 918  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON  21-11-2019   \n \nIdentification of Linguistic Minorities  \n918.    SHRI RAJU BISTA:  \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the process of identification of a community as a Linguistic Minority; \n(b)  the progress made on the recent request to “Preserve Heritage Minority Religious Sites” in the \nNorth Bengal particularly in the districts of Darjeeling, Kalimpong and North Dinajpur; and \n(c)  the steps taken by the Government to establish a “Dedicated Centre for Linguistic Minority \nEmpowerment” in Kurseong district of North Bengal? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) Linguistic Minorities have not been defined either in the Constitution of India or by the Statute.  \nHowever, Article 29 and 30 of the Constitution provide for protection of interest of minorities  \nwhich includes Linguistic Minorities  also.    \n \n            The working definition followed by the Commissioner for Linguistic Minorities   is as follows:- \n“Linguistic Minorities are group or collectivities of individuals residing in the territory of India or \nany part thereof having a distinct language or script of their own. The language of the minority \ngroup need not be one of the twenty two languages mentioned in the Eighth Schedule of the \nConstitution. In other words, linguistic minorities at the State level mean any group or groups \nof people whose mother tongues are different from the principal language of the State, and at \nthe district and taluka/tehsil levels, different from the principal language of the district or taluka/ \ntehsil concerned.” \n \nThe State/UT wise, broad linguistic profile is availabl e in the 52 nd Report of the Commissioner \nfor Linguistic Minorities (CLM). \n \n(b) There are two  centrally protected monuments namely (i) Tomb of Alexander-Cosma de Koros \nand (ii) Tomb of General Llhyod in the District of Darjeeling under Archeological Survey  of \nIndia (ASI).   There is no centrally protected monument in the District of Kalimpong and North \nDinajpur under ASI.    Day to day maintenance upkeep works of the aforesaid two monuments \nin Darjeeling District are undertaken.  \n \n(c)  No, Sir.  No such Centre for Linguistic Minority Empowerment in Kurseong district  has been \napproved by the Ministry of Minority Affairs. \n \n********** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |919", "t": "National Waterways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 919\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21\"rNovember,2019\nNATIONAL WATERWAYS\n919. SHRI SISIR KUMAR ADHIKARI:\nWill the Minister of SHlpplNG be pleased to state:\nqldcft-+q{{fi\n(a) whether it is a fact that the decision to declare 106 waterways of the country as ,'Natronal\nWaterways\" was met with strong opposition from the Centre's two major departments,\nthe NlTl Aayog and the Ministry of Finance;\n(b) if so, the details thereof including the objections raised by NlTl Aayog and the Ministry of\nFinance therefor;\n(c) the status of making these National Waterways operational and the quantum of funds\nearmarked for the purpose therein; and\n(d) the present status of interlinking of rivers in the country thereto?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR SHIPPING\n(SHRI MANSUKH MANDAVIYA)\n(a)& (b) ln addition to the five existing National Waterways (NWs), 106 new NWs were dectared\nunder the National waterways Act, 2016 which was notified on 12.04.2016. prior to the\ndeclaration of new NWs, under the proposed legislatron, inter Ministerial consultations, including\nwith NlTl Aayog and Ministry of Finance were held. The National Waterways Bill, 20 j 5 was also\ndeliberated by the Parliamentary Standing Committee on Transport, Tourism and Culture whrch\npresented its recommendations to Parliament in its 223'd Report. The National Waterways Bill,\n2015 was approved by Union Cabinet and passed by Parliament as National Wateryays Act,\n2016.\n(c) National Waterway (NW)-1 (Ganga-B h ag irathi-H oog h ty river system from Allahabad to\nHaldia), NW-2 (River Brahmaputra from Dhubri to sadiya), NW-3 (west coast canal from\nKottapuram to Kollam along with Udyogmandal and Champakara Canals) have been developed\nwith fairway navigational aids, jetties and terminals with mechanized equipment handting facilitiesfor loading and unloading of cargo. These NWs are operational and vessels are plyjng on them.\nln addition, NW-'10 (River Amba), NW-68 (River Mandovi), NW-73 (River Narmada), NW-83(Rajpuri creek), NW-85 (Revadanda creek-Kundalika River system), NW-91 (shastri river_Jaigad creek system), NW-97 (Sunderbans waterways), NW-1oo (River 'Tapi) and NW-111(River Zuari) are also operatlonal.\n\n-1-\nThe Jal Marg Vikas Project (JMVP) is being implemented at an estimated cost of Rs. 5369 18\ncrore for capacity augmentation of navigation on the Haldia-Varanasi stretch of NW-1 with the\ntechnical and financial assistance of the World Bank. Projects worth Rs. 1800 crore (approx )\nhave commenced on ground in a time period of three years after statutory clearances.\nFainvay development works on the vijayawada-puktyala stretch of river Krishna (part of NW-4)\nhas commenced at a cost of Rs. g6 crore.\nBased on the outcome of techno-economic feasibility and Detailed Project Reports (DpRs)\nprepared for 106 new NWs, technical interventions have been planned for safe navigation and\nshipping on technically viable NWs by the lnland Watenruays Authority of lndia (lWAl). As per\nthe DPRs completed so far for the new NWs, 36 NWs have been found to be technically viable\nand development activities have been initiated on 10 NWs viz. NW-g, NW-16, NW-27, NW-37,\nNW-40, NW-58, NW-68, NW-86, NW-97 and NW-111.\nTo develop the NWs, the Government provides funds to the lnland Waterurays Authority of lndia\nas Grants-in-aid and had permitted lWAl to raise Government of lndia fully serviced Bonds (Extra\nBudgetary Resources) of Rs. 1000 crore. ln this regard, the details of Budgetary Estimates,\nRevised Estimates and Expenditure during the last five years are given below:\n(Rs. in crore)\nYear Budget Estimate Revised\nimate I E'\n2015-16 323.19 (Grants) 321.91(Grants) 321\n2016-17 BE 391.01 (Grants)\n+\nEBRs 340.00\nE 362 31 (Grants) i \ngs8 4(,+l+\nEBRs 340.00 I 237.32 (\n26 oela G,*trl J \n- - q26 ogla\n+l+\n660 oo (EBR, I ___ 1rt_1?i?862.49 (Grants) i 862.49 ((\nl+I 111 94 (\n2017-18 270.00 (Grants)\n+\n660.00 (EBRs)\n201 B-1 I 480.00 (Grants)\n2019-20 750.36 (Grants) NA - f-3?4+Ttupto\n(d): The present status of interlinking of rivers in the country as reported by the Ministry of JalShakti is at Annex.\np.\n91\nGrants) --\nEBRs)\n(Ga;ia)-\n(EBRs)\n)2.49 (Grants)\n+\n11,91F_qR{\n30.09.2019)\n\n-)-\nAn nexu re\nThe National Perspective Plan (NPP) was prepared by the then Ministry of Irrigation (now\nMinistry of Jal Shakti) in August, 1980 for water resources development through inter basin\ntransfer of water, for transferring water from water surplus basins to water-deficit basins. Under\nthe NPP, the National Water Development Agency (NWDA) has identifie,J 30 links (16 under\nPeninsular Component and 14 under Himalayan Component) for preparation of ieasibility\nReports (FRs). The details of above river linking projects viz., rivers, States concerned, present\nstatus are given at Appendix.\nUnder the NPP, four priority links have also been identified for preparation of Detailed project\nReports (DPR) under the Peninsular Rivers component viz; Ken-Betwa link project (KB"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |92", "t": "Misuse of CSR Funds", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI,\nSIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH)\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.92\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND NOVEMBER, 2019\nAYUSH TREATMENT\n†*92. SHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) whether people in rural and urban areas are still dependent on Allopathy\ntreatment despite the promotion of AYUSH in recent years by the Government;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor;\n(c) whether it has been observed that there is higher usage of AYUSH treatment\nby men in urban areas than their rural counterparts whereas more women in\nrural areas use AYUSH treatment as compared to urban females; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and the action taken by the Government in this\nregard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE(IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF\nAYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND\nHOMOEOPATHY\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 92* FOR 22ND NOVEMBER, 2019\n(a) & (b) As reported by State/UT Governments, 17.74 Crores of patients reported\nto the AYUSH health care facilities during 2017 -18 compared to 15.94 Crores in\n2015-16 and 17.27 Crores of patients who availed AYUSH treatment during\n2016-17. The ef forts of Central Government for promotion of AYUSH through\nvarious programmes and schemes yielded in more number of people availing\ntreatment from AYUSH health care facilities. However, taking treatment from\nAllopathy or AYUSH is the choice of the people.\n(c) & (d) As per the finding of NSSO 71 st round on Social Consumption on Health\nin India conducted during January - June 2014, it has been noted that higher usage\n(1.5 percentage point) of such ‘Other’ treatment by urban male than its rural\ncounterpart while less usage of the same (0.8 percentage point) by urban female\nthan rural female. Moreover, un-treated spell was higher in rural (both for male and\nfemale) than urban.\nFurther, Government of India is implementing Centrally Sponsored Scheme\nNational AYUSH Mission (NAM) through States/UTs which envisages:\n(i) Better access to AYUSH services by supporting States/UTs for opening\nintegrated AYUSH hospitals, up -gradation of AYUSH hospitals and dispensaries,\nsupply of AYUSH medicines.\n(ii) Strengthening of AYUSH educational institutions by strengthening States/UTs\nto improve infrastructure of education institutions.\n(iii) Facilitate the enforcement of quality control of Ayurveda, Siddha and Unani &\nHomoeopathy (ASU&H) drugs by establishment of AYUSH pharmacies and drugs\ntesting laboratories.\n(iv) Sustainable availability of ASU & H raw -materials by encouraging cultivation\nof medicinal plants.\nIn addition, this Ministry has also taken various initiatives under the\nInformation Education and Comm unication (IEC) Scheme for propagation and\npromotion of AYUSH systems for treatment of common ailments through media\nand other publicity activities like organizing Arogya fairs, conferences, seminars\non AYUSH System. Further, Ministry is also supporting di fferent activities\nunder NAM like AYUSH gram, Public Health outreach activities, information\neducation and communication (IEC) activities, organization of Yoga,\nconducting peripheral OPD and Medical camps, behavioural change\ncommunication, training of vill age health workers towards identification and\nuse of local medicinal herbs. Grant-in-aid of Rs. 2125.63 Crores has been\nreleased to States/UTs for different activities of NAM since inception of NAM in\n2014 to till date. Yoga is being promoted in various ac tivities in Schools, work\nplaces, community as a whole to strengthen preventive and promotive Health."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |920", "t": "Audit of NRDWP", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.920  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2019  \n  \nAudit of NRDWP \n920. SHRI T.R. BAALU: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Performance Audit of the National Rural  Drinking Water Programme \n(NRDWP) for the period 2012-13 to 2016-17 has been conducted and report submitted \nby the Comptroller and Auditor General of India;  \n \n(b)if so, the details and the deficiencies pointed out by the Audit; \n(c) whether the targets of providingdrinking water by December, 2017 to allRural \nHabitations, Government Schools andAnganwadis to ha ve access to safe drinkingwater, \nhave been achieved; \n(d)if so, the details thereof,State-wise; and \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA) \n \n(a) Yes Sir.  \n(b) Performance audit report of CAG has made some o bservations which may be \ncategorized into the following:  \ni.) Planning related observations: Non-preparation of state specific sector policy \nframework, water security plan, delayed submission ofannual action plans, non-\nconstitution of State Water and Sanitation Mission (SWSM) and not conducting \nperiodic State Level Scheme Sanctioning Committee (SLSSC), etc. \nii.) Fund management related observations: Some sta tes either did not release \ntheir share or delayed the releases, delayed submis sion of proposals for release \nof fund &utilization certificates by States, stipul ated fund flow mechanism was \nnot followed, fund released by the Centre and the S tate were not routed \nthrough SWSM, etc. \n\niii.) Programme implementation related observations : Delay in completion of \nschemes, lack of coordination, knowledge & skill an d non-preparation/ \nimplementation of support activity plans etc. \niv.) Monitoring and Evaluation related observations : Non-functioning of \nschemes, non-formation of Village Water & Sanitatio n Committee, lack of \ncoverage in institutions, Data discrepancy and inco nsistency etc. as revealed by \nevaluation studies. \n(c) & (d) Under erstwhile National Rural Drinking W ater Programme (NRDWP), as \nreported by States/ UTs, as on 01.04.2018 out of to tal 17.25 lakh rural habitations, \ninteralia  covering Government schools and anganwadis,13.61 l akh habitations were \nfully covered with provision of 40 litre or more pe r capita per day (lpcd) and around 3 \nlakh habitations were partially covered, i.e. with provision of less than 40 lpcd whereas \n64,743 habitations were having water quality issues.  \n(e) Does not arise.  \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |921", "t": "Low Representation of Women", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUN-STARRED QUESTION NO.921 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019 \n \nLOW REPRESENTATION OF WOMEN \n \n921.     SHRI SHIVAKUMAR C. UDASI \n \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether India ranked low globally in terms of representation of women in \nExecutive Government and Parliament and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the number of women in Executive Gov ernment and in Parliament \nworldwide has stagnated, with only a marginal improvement and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n(c) whether the Government is considering Taiwan model in terms of empowering \nwomen as leaders in politics and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n \n(a) to (c) : In the 2019 Lok Sabha elections, 724 women contested election, out of which \n78 were elected. The present ratio of women representation in Lok Sabha \nstands at 14.42%. This is higher than 2014 elections in which only 68 women \nwere elected to the Lok Sabha.  Similarly, in the Rajya Sabha, women \nrepresentation stands at 25 as on 20 th November, 2019. In addition, in the \npresent Council of Ministers, there are 6 women Ministers.  \nIt has been the endeavor of the Government to provide for adequate \nrepresentation in the Lok Sabha as well as in the State Legislative \nAssemblies.  With the objective of empowe ring women as leaders in politics, \nGovernment of India has been undertaking capacity building programmes for \nElected Women representatives of Panchayati Raj Institutions so that they \nserve as catalysts of social change. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |922", "t": "Field Trials of Genetically Modified Brinjal and Mustard", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 922 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019  \n \nField Trials of Genetically Modified Brinjal and Mustard \n \n922. SHRI KIRTI VARDHAN SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a) whether the Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC) has put on hold the \ndecision of field trials of GM-Brinjal since 2009 and GM-Mustard since 2018 despite \nhaving no scientific evidence to prove that these crops are unsafe; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to prepares a road map for conducting field trials \nand for further introduction of transgenic crops in the near future; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n(a) and (b) Stakeholders have expressed concerns regarding health and environment \nsafety of Genetically Modified (GM) Brinjal and GM Mustard. Hence, \nGenetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC) has recommended \nadditional studies regarding impact on environment, health, risk to local \nvarieties etc.   \n \n(c) and (d) GEAC is a regulator and discharges its responsibilities regarding \nenvironmental release of GM crops based on scientific evidence. All \napplicants interested in introducing GM crops have to undertake elaborate \nfood, feed and environmental safety assessment following regulatory \nguidelines and standard operating procedures under Rules for the \nManufacture, Use/Import/Export and Storage of Hazardous Micro Organisms/ \nGenetically Engineered Organisms or Cells, 1989 of Environment (Protection) \nAct, 1986, before environment release for cultivation.        \n \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |923", "t": "Single Regulatory Body in Health Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.923\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND NOVEMBER, 2019\nSINGLE REGULATORY BODY IN HEALTH SECTOR\n923. SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to bring a single authority framework to oversee\nfunctioning and streamlining regulatory structure for present three independent regulators\nworking separately for food, drugs and devices in health sector; and\n(b) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a): No. There is no such proposal.\n(b): Does not arise.\n…………………."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |924", "t": "NGOs Assistance to Deserted Brides", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \n LOK SABHA \n \nUN-STARRED QUESTION NO.924 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019 \n \n  NGOs ASSISTANCE TO DESERTED BRIDES \n \n \n924. MS. DIYA KUMARI : \n \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total number of NGO’s associated with scheme for providing legal/ \nfinancial assistance to Indian women deserted by their overseas Indian (NRI)/ \nforeigner husbands;  \n(b) the procedure pr escribed by the Government to empanel an NGO under the \nscheme; and  \n(c) the total number of beneficiaries under the scheme? \n \n \nANSWER  \n \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) Under the ‘Indian Community Welfare Fund (ICWF) Scheme’ set up by \nMinistry of External Affairs, 193 Organisations/ NGOs are registered with \nIndian Missions and Posts abroad.   \n(b) The procedure prescribed by the Government to empanel an NGO under the \nscheme is given at annexure-I. \n(c)  From January, 2016 to October, 2019, Indian Missions and Posts abroad \nhave provided assistance to 81 Indian women under ICWF. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nCRITERION FOR EMPANELMENT OF NGOs UNDER THESCHEME \n \n \nThe following information/documents may be supplied, inter alia, by Indian \nMissions/Posts while proposing empanelment of Indian Women’s \nOrganizations, Indian Community Associations or NGOs (here after \nreferred to as organization) for providing legal and fi nancial assistance to \nIndian women deserted by their overseas Indianspouses: \n \n1. Name, address and contact details of theorganization \n2. Year of its establishment (documentary proof to be attached) \n3. Principal field(s) ofactivity/operation \n4. Managing Committee/Governing Body/Office Bearers – provide the \ncurrentlist \n5. Total membership in the last three years, yearwise \n6. Principal sources of itsfunding \n7. Audited accounts for the last threeyears \n8. Particulars of legal consultancy firm, if any, of the organization \n9. Name and particulars of women legal practitioners, if any, who are \nmembers of theorganization \n10. Major initiatives, if any, taken by theorganization \n11. Main services, if any, rendered to the IndianCommunity \n12. Major Charity activities undertaken in the last threeyears \n13. Activities, if any, undertaken specifically for the welfare of women \nbelonging to the Indiancommunity \n14. Has it been associated in rendering assistance to Indian women \ndeserted by their overseas Indianspouses? \n15. How many cases the organization would be able to take up in ayear? \n16. Recommendations of the Head of theMission/Post? \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |925", "t": "Juvenile Homes", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.925 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019 \n \nJUVENILE HOMES \n \n 925.  SHRI D. K. SURESH: \n Shri L. S. TEJASVI SURYA: \n \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Ministry has been able to assess the number of beneficiaries of the \nJuvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015, in the country and \nspecific to the State of Karnataka and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b)  whether any irregularities have been found in providing basic needs mentioned \nunder the Act to the affected Juveniles and if so, whether the Government has \nconducted any surprise checks/inspection at special homes established under \nthe Provision of Juvenile Justice (Care and Pr otection of Children) Act, 2015, \nand details thereof;  \n(c)  the details of the rehabilitation services in Karnataka that the Ministry has \nprovided under the Child Protection Services Scheme, since the \nimplementation of the said Act in 2015;  \n(d)  whether t he Government intends to allocate certain additional funds towards \nproviding the rehabilitation services to all States under the Act and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and  \n(e)  the number of cases pending with District Magistrates or courts regarding the \npassing of adoption order State-wise including Bengaluru? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n(a) to (d): The provisions of Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015 ( JJ \nAct) applies to all matters concerning “Children in Need of Care and Protection” (CNCP) and \n“Children in Conflict with Law” (CCL).  The children under CCL and CNCP are defined under \nSection 2(13) and 2(14) respectively of the JJ Act. The primary respons ibility of execution of \nthe Act lies with the State/UTs. However, t he Ministry of Women and Child Development is \nimplementing a centrally sponsored Scheme i.e. „Child Protection Services (CPS)‟ (erstwhile \nIntegrated Child Protection Scheme) for supporting the children in difficult circumstances . \nThe primary responsibility  of implementation of the scheme lies with the State \nGovernments/UT Administrations. Details of Child Care Institutions in the country along with \nnumber of Children residing in these institutions under Child Protection Services (CPS) as \nprovided by State Governments/UT Administrations including Karnataka is at Annexure-I \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nThe JJ Act and the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Model Rules, 2016 \nframed thereunder  inter-alia specifies the benchmark of services to be delivered to the \nchildren. The monitoring mechanism is prescribed under Section 54 of the JJ Act and Rule \n41 of the Model Rules, 2016. The Ministry has been urging the State Governments and UT \nAdministrations from time to time to register all the CCIs under the provisions of JJ Act, so as \nto ensure that CCIs offer optimum services not less than the minimum standard of care and \nprotection prescribed under the Act & Rules. Section 39 of the JJ Act, provide s information \nabout process of rehabilitation and social re-integration.  \n \nThe Ministry has been perusing all the State Governments and UT Administrations to issue \ndirections for inspection of all CCIs under the supervision of District Magistrates in each \ndistrict. The Ministry has also issued an advisory to the States and UTs regarding the action \nto be taken in case of disruption to the life of children in case of any untoward incidence of \nabuse in any CCI. Under CPS , financial assistance is provided to St ate Governments/UT \nAdministrations with the objective to create a safe and secure environment for overall \ndevelopment and welfare of children in need of care and protection and children in conflict \nwith law.  \n \n(e): As per Central Adoption Resource Authorit y (CARA), the State-wise number of adoption \ncases pending in various Family and District Courts and details of adoption cases pending in \nFamily/District Courts of Bengaluru is at Annexure-II. \n \n*** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure-I \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No.925 for answer on \n22.11.2019 raised by Shri D. K. Suresh and Shri L. S. Tejasvi Surya regarding ‘Juvenile Homes’. \n \nDetails of Child Care Institutions in the country along with number of Children residing in these \ninstitutions under CPS as on 15.11.2019. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n    Institutional Care [Homes] Open Shelters Specialised Adoption \nAgencies \nS. \nNo. State No. Assisted Beneficiaries No. \nAssisted Beneficiaries No. \nAssisted Beneficiaries \n1 Andhra Pradesh 66 2954 9 262 14 140 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 5 185 0 0 1 10 \n3 Assam 37 1765 3 51 23 69 \n4 Bihar 26 1567 5 134 13 138 \n5 Chhattisgarh 65 2325 10 117 12 120 \n6 Goa 23 1188 3 378 2 16 \n7 Gujarat 45 1706 0 0 12 86 \n8 Haryana 24 1322 14 425 7 52 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 32 1268 4 91 1 15 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |926", "t": "FM Radio Station in Gujarat", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n MINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 926 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019) \nFM RADIO STATION IN GUJARAT \n926. SHRI PRADEEP KUMAR SINGH: \nSHRIMATI RATHVA GITABEN VAJESINGBHAI: \nSHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be \npleased to state:  \n(a)  the number of radio stations set up in Gujarat State; \n(b)  whether the Government has taken any steps to improve the \nradio signals in rural areas of Gujarat; and  \n(c)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE; MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; \nAND MINISTER OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC \nENTERPRISES  \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n\n \n(a): Prasar Bharati has informed that presently, there are 16 AIR \nstations functioning in the State of Gujarat  at Ahmedabad, Ahwa, \nBhuj, Godhra, Himmatnagar, Rajkot, Surat, Vadodara, Mehsana, \nBhavnagar, Amreli, Bharuch, Jamnagar, Porbandar, Dwarka  and \nJunagarh.  \n(b) & (c):  Prasar Bharati is providing radio coverage to most of \nthe areas including rural areas of Gujarat. To improve the radio \nsignals in various parts of Gujarat,  Prasar Bharati has \nimplemented the replacement of outlived MW transmitter s by \nstate-of-the-art digit al transmitter s at Ahmedabad and Rajkot . \nReplacement and upgradation of FM transmitter s at Ahmedabad \nand Godhra has also been implemented. \nIn addition to above, a 10 kW FM station  with studio facility at \nDahod and 100 W FM transmitters at 10 locations have also been \napproved. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |927", "t": "CGHS Hospitals/Wellness Centres", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.927\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND NOVEMBER, 2019\nCGHS HOSPITALS/WELLNESS CENTRES\n927. SHRI NATARAJAN P.R.:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is aware of the fact that the employees and pensioners are in need of\nCGHS Hospital/ Wellness Centre facilities in more places to avoid upfront payment for their\ntreatment;\n(b) if so, the details of decision taken or likely to be taken to open additional CGHS wellness\ncentres across the country;\n(c) the details of fund provisioned/ allocated during each year of the past five years including\ncurrent year for the purpose; and\n(d) if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a): Medical treatment facilities to Central Government employees are provided by the\nGovernment either by extending the benefits of Central Government Health Scheme (CGHS) or\nthrough the mechanism under Central Services (Medical Attendance) [CS(MA)] Rules, 1944. In\nboth cases, the Government reimburses to the government employee the cost of hospitalization at\nempanelled hospitals. In case of OPD treatment, howe ver, the beneficiary does not have to pay\nanything at CGHS Wellness Centre, whereas the cost of OPD treatment given by Authorised\nMedical Attendant under CS(MA) Rules, 1944 is reimbursed by the office of the government\nemployee concerned.\nIn case of Centr al Government Pensioners residing in CGHS areas, medical treatment\nfacilities, both Indoor and Outdoor, are provided by CGHS within parameters of the scheme.\nCentral Government Pensioners residing in non -CGHS covered areas can avail Fixed\nMedical Allowanc e every month. They also have the option to avail CGHS facility from the\nnearest CGHS covered city on payment of requisite subscription.\n\n-2-\n(b): In 2014, CGHS was operational in 25 cities with 273 Allopathic Wellness Centres. The\nsame is now extended to 71 cities with 328 Allopathic Wellness Centres.  Details are at\nAnnexure.\n(c) & (d): There is no separate plan scheme regarding opening of new CGHS Wellness Centre.\nHence, no separate budget is provisioned for opening new CGHS Wellness Centres. The\nexpenditure for this purpose is incurred from the overall budget of the Ministry of Health &\nFamily Welfare allocated to CGHS.\n………………\n\nAnnexure\nDetails of CGHS Wellness Centres across the country\nYear CGHS covered cities No. of Allopathic Wellness Centre\n2014 25 273\n2019 71 328\nN.B.: Satellite towns of National Capital Region are considered as part of Delhi for\nadministrative purposes."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |928", "t": "HHEC", "head": " \n \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 928 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019 \n \nHHEC \n \n928.         SHRIMATI JYOSTNA CHARANDAS MAHANT: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंरी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether Handicrafts an Handloom Exports Corporation of  India Limited (HHEC) have \nsought the views of Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) with regard to withholding the payments \nof small bullion parties against whom no case or investigation is pending; \n \n(b) if so, the complete details thereof and reasons for not giving views/clarification by the CBI to \nHHEC: and ; \n \n(c) whether CBI would expedite and give the views as the payment of bullion parities depend on \ntheir clarification/ opinion/findings, if not the reason therefor? \n \n उत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र मंरी (श्रीमती स्मृतत ज़ूबिन इरानी) \nMINISTER OF TEXTILES \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (c): Yes Sir. Central Bureau of I nvestigation (CBI) is conducting investigation in a  case \nagainst M/s Edelweiss Commodities Limited (ECL), CBI has examined the transaction  of buyer’s \nCredit and sought information from The Handicrafts & Handlooms Exports Corporation of India \nLimited (HHEC)  regarding availing of buyer’s credit and passing o n difference amount between \ninterest earned on Fixed Deposit (FD) and interest charged by the foreign bank for providing buyer’s \ncredit. The case has not been concluded as yet. HHEC has withheld payments  of ECL against which \nCBI investigation is going on. The company has also withheld amounts of  other bullion parties with \nwhom HHEC has identical agreements as signed with ECL . The investigation by CBI in the case of \nECL may have implications on similar transaction. Further, HHEC has sought the views of CBI on \nwithholding of payments of other bullion parties but no response has been received from CBI. The \nrelease of payment to other bullion parties will depend on the outcome of CBI Investigation against \nECL. \n.         \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |929", "t": "Kyoto Protocol", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 929 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019  \n \nKyoto Protocol \n \n929.  SHRI RAJAN VICHARE: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether India and China have jointly urged rich nations to ratify the second commitment \nperiod of the Kyoto Protocol (2013-2020) guiding their climate action for next three \nyears by June 2020 to build trust in the multilateral process; and \n(b)  if so, the details and facts thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n(a) and (b): India participated in the 29th Ministerial meeting of the BASIC (Brazil, South Africa, \nIndia, and China) countries on Climate Change held in Beijing, China on 25th -26th October \n2019. The meeting was attended by all member states including India and China. \n \nAt the conclusion of the meeting, the BASIC group underscored Kyoto Protocol as a \nsignificant milestone in the multilateral climate process. The group urged Parties, that have not \nyet ratified the Doha Amendment (second commitment period) to do so as soon as possible, \nstriving for its prompt entry into force before the 25th Conference of Parties to the United \nNations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC).  \n \nThe Group also urged developed countries to take urgent actions to close the gaps, \nincluding revisiting their targets on mitigation under the UNFCCC and the Kyoto Protocol, and \nfulfilling their commitments of providing support to developing countries.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |93", "t": "Kerala Bank", "head": "���������� �� �����\n�������� �� �������\n��� �����\n��������� �������� ��� ��\n�������� �� ������������������ ��� ���� ������\n������ ����\n93. ADV. DEAN KURIAKOSE:\nSHRI THOMAS CHAZHIKADAN:\nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Union Government has permitted the Government of Kerala to start a\nscheduled bank called ‘Kerala Bank’;\n(b) if so, the details thereof including the details of permission/clearance granted by the RBI\nin this regard;\n(c) whether the RBI has conducted any study to analyze the impact on primary cooperative\nbanks due to the introduction of Kerala bank; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE\n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR)\n(a) & (b): Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has reported that it has accorded final approval to the\nproposal of the Government of Kerala to amalgamate with the Kerala State Cooperative\nBank Ltd, thirteen District Central Cooperative Banks (DCCBs) in Kerala (Thiruvananthpuram,\nKollam, Alappuzha, Idukki, Ernakulam, Kottayam, Pathanamthitta, Thrissur, Palakkad,\nKozhikode, Wayanad, Kannur and Kasaragod) that have approved the Scheme of\nAmalgamation (SOA) at least by a simple majority in their General Body Meeting. However,\nthe said approval shall be contingent upon the Hon’ble High Court of Kerala upholding the\nvalidity of the amendment made to the provisions of Section 14A of the Kerala Co-operative\nSocieties Act and Government of Kerala complying with certain conditions stipulated by RBI.\nThe approval shall be valid for a period of six months till March 31, 2020.\n(c) & (d): RBI has reported that no study to analyse the impact on primary co-operative\nbanks due to the introduction of Kerala Bank has been conducted by them.\n*******"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |930", "t": "Radio Station in Bengal", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n MINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 930 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019) \nRADIO STATION IN BENGAL \n930. SHRI SHANTANU THAKUR: \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be \npleased to state:  \n(a)  the number of radio stations set up in West Bengal including \nthe Constituency of Bangaon;  \n(b)  whether the Government has taken any steps to improve the \nradio signals in rural areas of West Bengal; and  \n(c)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE; MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; \nAND MINISTER OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC \nENTERPRISES  \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n(a): Prasar Bharati has informed that there are 15 AIR stations  \n(MW/FM) in West Bengal . There is no AIR Setup in Bangaon \n\nConstituency. However, MW Signals from Kolkata and Chinsurah \nTransmitters are available in Bangaon Parliamentary \nConstituency. \nIn so far as private FM is concerned, presently 14 private FM radio \nstations are operational in 3 cities, viz. Kolkata (8), Asansol (3) \nand Siliguri (3) in West Bengal. \n(b) & (c):  Prasar Bharati is providing radio coverage to most of \nthe areas of West Bengal  including rural areas . To improve the \nradio signals in the state, Prasar Bharati has implemented \nreplacement of outlived transmitters by state -of-the-art digital \n(DRM) transmitters at Kolkata, Siliguri, Chinsurah and by FM \ntransmitters at Kurseong, Krishnan agar and Balurghat \nrespectively.  \nIn addition to this FM stations at Cooch Behar and Bardhaman \nhave been approved as also a FM transmitter at Allipurdwar, \nDistrict Jalpaiguri.  \nVarious AIR channels, including c hannels originating from West \nBengal, are also streamed digitally, and are available on Prasar \nBharati Website www.prasarbharati.gov.in and also on mobile app \n“NewsOnAir” available on Android and iOS platform. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |931", "t": "Regulations on Culling of Vermin Species", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 931. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019. \n \nRegulations on Culling of Vermin Species \n \n \n931.  SHRI UTTAM KUMAR REDDY NALAMADA: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether there are any regulations on the culling of vermin species to ensure that  their \npopulations are protected and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the Government proposes to establish a body to oversee the inclusion of \nspecies in the list of vermin species and to ensure that culling is carried out sustainably \nand if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether there are any measures in place to ensure that there is no collateral damage to \nother species due to measures such as electric fences, traps, snares which are set up to \nprotect human interests from vermin species; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the other measures taken by the Government to reduce human-wildlife conflicts? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n(a)& (b) The Section 62 of the   Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972 provides that the Central \nGovernment may, by notification, declare any wild animal other than those \nspecified in Schedule I and Part II of Schedule II to be vermin for any area and for \nsuch period as may be specified therein and so long as such notification is in force, \nsuch wild animal shall be deemed to have been included in Schedule V.  \n \nThe Central Government on receipt of proposals from the State Governments, \ngiving justification for declaring any wild animals as vermin, is competent to notify \nsuch wild animal as vermin. The Ministry in the past has notified some of the wild \nanimals as vermin on receipt of the request of the State Government.    \n \n \n\n \n(c)  & (d) As per the Wild Life Protection Act, 1972 hunting including  capturing, coursing, \nsnaring, trapping, driving or baiting any wild or captive animal  is prohibited under \nSection 11 of the Wildlife protection Act 1972.   \n \n \n(e) \n \nThe measures taken by the Government to reduce human-wildlife conflicts are as \nfollows: \n \ni. Ministry through its Centrally Sponsored Schemes (CSS) namely ‘Project      \nTiger’, ‘Project Elephant’ and ‘Development of Wildlife Habitats (DWH)’ \nprovides funds to States and UTs for carrying various activities for habitat \nimprovement works namely restoration of natural water bodies, creation of \nartificial ponds, waterholes, augmenting food/fodder sources at various places \nwithin Protected Areas to minimize the human wildlife conflict. \n \nii. A network of Protected Areas namely viz., National Park, Sanctuaries, \nConservation Reserves and Community Reserves covering important wildlife \nhabitat have been created all over the country under the provisions of the Wild \nLife (Protection) Act, 1972 to conserve wild animals and their habitats. \n \niii. Construction/erecting of physical barriers, such as barbed wire fence, solar \npowered electric fence, bio-fencing using cactus, boundary wall etc. to prevent \nthe entry of wild animals into crop field. \n \niv. The Ministry has approved a project for undertaking ‘Immuno-contraceptive \nmeasures’ for population management of wild animals. \n \nv. The Ministry has issued guidelines in context of human-wildlife conflict to the \nChief Wildlife Wardens of all the State Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations dated 24th December, 2014 and 1st June, 2015. \n \nvi. To mitigate wildlife conflict along the linear infrastructures like rail tracks, \nroads/highways and power transmission lines passing through the Protected \nAreas and other wildlife rich areas, Standing Committee of National Board for \nWildlife has recommended that all linear infrastructure development \nAgencies, shall submit wild animals passage plan based on the WII Guideline \n“Eco-Friendly Measures to Mitigate Impacts of linear infrastructure on \nWildlife”. These guideline suggest for modification in the designs of the linear \ninfrastructures by way of providing eco-friendly structure which will ensure \nsafe movement of wildlife across these linear infrastructures. \n \nvii. Periodic awareness campaigns to sensitize guide and advise the general public \non man-animal conflict, including dissemination of information through \nvarious forms of media. \n**** \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |932", "t": "Ayushman Bharat Beneficiaries in Ludhiana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.932\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND NOVEMBER, 2019\nAYUSHMAN BHARAT BENEFICIARIES IN LUDHIANA\n932. SHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of beneficiaries covered by the Ayushman Bharat Programme till date in\nLudhiana;\n(b) the percentage of claims that have been denied under the scheme till date in Ludhiana;\n(c) whether the Government is aware that Ludhiana has a shortage of doctors which leads to\nhuge waiting period for people; and\n(d) if so, the steps taken to improve the situation?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a): Facilities under Ayushman Bharat – Health and Wellness Centres are free and universal to\nall. The total number of beneficiary families covered under Ayushman Bharat Pradhan Mantri\nJan Arogya Yojana (AB-PMJAY) in Ludhiana as per SECC data and state scheme is 56,941.\n(b): As per information received from State Health Agency of Punjab, as on 18. 11.2019 the\ntotal claims raised by empanelled hospitals under the scheme in Ludhiana were 3,013, out of these\n23 were rejected that is  0.76% of the claims raised.\n(c)  & (d) : Health being a state subject, no data related to number of doctors district -wise is\nmaintained at the central level either by Medical Council of India or by the Government of India.\nHowever, under AB-PMJAY 63 hospitals have been empanelled in Ludhiana. Out of these,\n47 are privates and 16 are public hospitals.\n……………….."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |933", "t": "Anganwadi Workers", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 933 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019 \n \nANGANWADI WORKERS \n \n933.  ADV. A.M. ARIFF:  \nSHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE:  \nDR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL:  \nDR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE:  \n \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total number of Anganwadi workers/helpers working in the rural and urban \nareas of the country, State-wise along with their duties and pay/salary;  \n(b) whether the Government has provided the basic facilities for these Anganwadi \nworkers and children under their care and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether Government has received grievances/complaints from them and if so, \nthe details thereof along with the remedial steps taken by Government; \n(d) whether Government is aware of the recommendations of the 45 thSession of \nIndian Labour Conference regarding basic minimum wages and regular \nemployment of health workers; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof, if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) State-wise number of Anganwadi Workers/Anganwadi Helpers working in the \ncountry is at Annexure-I. The Anganwadi Services (under Umbrella ICDS ) \nScheme envisages the Anganwadi Workers & Helpers as honorary workers from \nlocal community who come forward to render their services, on part time basis, in \nthe area of child care and development. Duties and Resp onsibilities of \nAWWs/AWHs are at Annexure-II.  \nBeing honorary work ers, they are paid monthly honora rium as decided by \nGovernment from time to time. The Government of India has recently enhanced \nthe honorarium of Anganwadi Workers (AWWs) from Rs. 3,000/ - to Rs. 4,500/ - \nper month; AWWs at mini -AWCs from Rs. 2,250/ - to Rs. 3,500/- per month; \nAnganwadi Helpers (AWHs) from Rs 1,500/ - to Rs. 2,250/ - per month; and \nintroduced performance linked incentive  of Rs. 250/ - per month to AWHs,w.e.f.  \n1stOctober, 2018. Further, AWWs are allowed performance linked incentive of ₹ \n500/- per month for using ICDS-CAS under POSHAN Abhiyaan.In addition to the \nhonorarium paid by the Government of India, the respective State/UTs giv e \nmonetary incentives to these workers out of their own resources  as per details \ngiven at Annexure-III.  \n  \n\n \n(b) Anganwadi Workers (AWWs) and Anganwadi Helpers (AWHs) are entitled for \npaid absence of 180 days of maternity leave , Awards at the National and State \nlevel, two sets of uniform per year, insurance coverage under the Pradhan Mantri \nJeevan JyotiBimaYojana  (for 18 -50 y ears age functionaries), Pradhan Mantri \nSuraksha BimaYojana  (for 18 -59 years age functionaries) and \nAnganwadiKaryakartiBimaYojana (for 53 -59 years age functionaries for the \nInsurance Cycle 2019-20). \nUnder the Anganwadi Services scheme, children are provide d multiple services \nsuch as Supplementary Nutrition (SNP), Pre-school Non -formal Education, \nNutrition & Health Education, Immunization, Health Check -up, and Referral \nServices. Other facilities available to Children under Anganwadi  Services include \nprovision of toilet and safe drinking water facilities, Learning Modules, etc. \n(c) Several grievances/complaints are being received in this Ministry from time to \ntime.Since the Anganwadi Services is a Centrally Sponsored Scheme and the \nadministration, management & mon itoring including recruitment of field \nfunctionaries are being done by the concerned State Government/UT \nAdministration.  Hence , the grievances/complaints are forwarded to them for \ntaking remedial measures. \n(d) & (e) The recommendations of Conference Committee  on “Service conditions, \nwages and social security for various categories of workers employed in Central \nGovernment and State Government schemes (Anganwadi, Mid -Day Meal, \nSarvaShikshaAbhiyan and other schemes) under various Ministries of Central \nGovernment” was included in 45 th Indian L abour Conference. The details of the \nrecommendations for Anganwadi Workers/Helpers made are as under: \n \ni. They should be first recognised as “workers” and not volunteers or honorary \nworkers; \nii. They should be paid minimum wages; \niii. They should get all social security benefits like pension, gratuity, maternity \nbenefits etc.; \niv. To begin with, the social security schemes for unorganised workers like \nAamAadmiBimaYojana (AABY) and RashtriyaSwasthyaBimaYojana (RSBY) \nshould be extended to these workers; \nv. Anganwadi Centres should be in pucca building in good conditions with all \nbasic amenities. Similarly, for ASHA workers also, suitable working space \nwith basic amenities should be provided; \nvi. They should have right to organize and collective bargaining; \nvii. Respective Departments should formulate “Employment Standing Order” for \nthese workers to regulate their employment and service conditions (wherever \nnot existing); \nviii. For Anganwadi Workers and Helpers, who h"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |934", "t": "Counterfeit Drugs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.934\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND NOVEMBER, 2019\nCOUNTERFEIT DRUGS\n934. MOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P.:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is aware about the fact that up to 20 per cent of drugs sold in India\nare counterfeit;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether any efforts have been taken by the Governmen t to eradicate counterfeit drugs in the\ncountry; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a) & (b): No. There is no such data with the Ministry.\nIn order to ensure the quality of drugs in the country, both the Central Drugs Standard\nControl Organisation (CDSCO) and the State drug regulators pick up a large number of samples\nof drugs from all over the country and get them tested and analysed in the laboratories of the\nCentral and State Governments. In a few cases, the samples tested and analysed do not meet the\nprescribed standards. The details of the drugs that do not meet the standards are immediately\nnotified by the Central or State regulator concerned for necessary action.\n(c) & (d): The Government is committed to ensuring that the quality, safety and efficacy of\ndrugs are not compromised. With this in view, the Government has taken a series of measures\nincluding strengthening of legal provisions, workshops and training programmes for\nmanufacturers and regulatory officials and measures such as risk based inspections.\n…………………"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |935", "t": "Third Party Evaluation", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 935 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019 \n \n THIRD PARTY EVALUATION \n \n935. SHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY: \n  \n            Will the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n   \n(a) whether the Government is planning to conduct a third party evaluation on the \nimplementation of Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana Scheme in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the total amount sanctioned under this scheme so far to the State of Andhra \nPradesh during the last two financial years and the number of beneficiaries who \navailed the scheme, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b) As decided by the Government, NITI Aayog is getting the third party evaluation \nconducted of Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (PMMVY). \n \n(c)  Under PMMVY, funds amounting to ₹7022.36 Lakh  and ₹14,101.75 Lakh were \nsanctioned/ released to the Government of Andhra Pradesh during the financial years \n2017-18 and 2018 -19 respectively. The total number of beneficiaries availed during the \nyear 2017 -18 and 2018 -19 in the State of Andhra Pradesh are  1,53,099 and 5,00,361 \nrespectively. \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |936", "t": "Vegetable Cultivation in Anganwadi Centres", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUN STARRED QUESTION NO. 936 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019 \n \nVEGETABLE CULTIVATION IN ANGANWADI CENTRES \n \n936.  SHRI KANUMURU RAGHU RAMA KRISHANA RAJU: \n  SHRI B. B. PATIL:    \n       \n Will the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether Anganwadi staff is being provided adequate salaries/remuneration, \nsmart phones, medical facilities and nutritional food across the country and if so, \nthe details thereof, State/UT-wise including Telangana; \n \n(b)  whether the Government is in the process of developing a software with the help \nof Aadhaar to prepare a common application to provide nutrition to beneficiaries \nlike poor and pregnant women  under Integrated Child Dev elopment Services \n(ICDS); \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d)  whether the Government is also planning to introduce vegetable cultivation in \nAnganwadi Centres with an aim to provide healthy food for children; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (c) The Anganwadi Services {under Umbrella Integrated Child Development \nServices (ICDS) Scheme} envisages the Anganwadi Workers & Helpers as \nhonorary workers from local co mmunity who come forward to render their \nservices, on part time basis, in the area of child care  and development. Being \nhonorarium workers, they are paid monthly honoraria as decided by Government \nfrom time to time.  \n  The Government of India has recently enhanced the honorarium of \nAnganwadi Workers (AWWs) at main -Anganwadi Centres (AWCs) from Rs. \n3,000/- to Rs. 4,500/ - per month; AWWs at mini -AWCs from Rs. 2,250/ - to Rs. \n3,500/- per month; Anganwadi Helpers (AWHs) from Rs 1,500/ - to Rs. 2,250/ - \nper month; and introduced performance linked incentive of Rs. 250/ - per month \nto AWHs, effective from 1 st October, 2018. AWWs are paid Rs. 500/ - per month \nunder POSHAN Abhiyaan for using ICDS -CAS. Besides this, many States/UTs \nare also giving additional honorarium to  Anganwadi Workers and Anganwadi \nHelpers out of their own resources for any additional work assigned to them. \nStatement indicating additional honorarium paid by States/UTs to AWWs/AWHs \nis placed at Annexure-I. \n\n  Under the Social Security benefits, Anganwad i Workers (AWWs)/ \nAnganwadi Helpers (AWHs) in the age group of 18 -50 years are covered under \nPradhan Mantri Jeevan Jyoti Bima Yojana (PMJJBY) providing life cover of ₹ \n2.00 Lakh, those in the age group of 18 -59 years  are covered under  Pradhan \nMantri Suraksha Bima Yojana (PMSBY) for accidental cover of ₹ 2.00 Lakh / ₹ \n1.00 Lakh and those in the age group of 51 -59 years (closed group as on \n01.06.2017) are covered with the  modified Anganwadi Karyakarti Bima Yojana \n(AKBY) for life cover of ₹ 30,000/ -. The AWWs/AWHs are also provided Female \nCritical Illness benefits of ₹ 20,000/ - on diagnosis of identified illness and \nscholarships to their children studying in 9th to 12th Standard (including ITI \ncourses). These social security benefits to AWWs/AWHs are being prov ided in \nassociation with the LIC.  \n  AWWs and AWHs are also entitled for Supplementary Nutrition under the \nAnganwadi Services Scheme. The scale of nutrition is the same as that for \nPregnant Women & Lactating Mothers. \n  POSHAN Abhiyaan  empowers the frontline functionaries i.e. Anganwadi \nWorkers and Lady Supervisors by providing them with smart phones. ICDS -\nCommon Application Software  /(ICDS-CAS) have been developed under \nPOSHAN Abhiyaan which enables data capture, ensures assigned servi ce \ndelivery and prompts for interventions wherever required. It enables collection of \ninformation on ICDS service delivery interventions and its impact on nutrition \noutcomes in beneficiaries.  \n  This data/information is then available on web -based ICDS -CAS \ndashboard on a near real time basis, for monitoring , to the supervisory staff at \nBlock, District, State and  National level for action and decision making at their  \nend. The dashboard displays data and provides reports on various programme \nareas to the administrative officers to interpret and use the information  for \nimproving service delivery. As on 17.11.2019, 5,26,869 Anganwadi Workers are \nusing the ICDS -CAS Applic ation across 36 States/UTs as per details in \nAnnexure-II.  \n \n(d)     & (e) States/UTs are encouraged by the Government of India to develop kitchen \ngardens at the Anganwadi Centre premises to provide healthy food for children. \n \n********* \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nStatement referred to in part (a) of Lok Sabha Un-Starred Question No. 936 \nto be answered on 22.11.2019  \n* Depending on the qualification and/or number of years of service  \nS. \nNo. \nStates/UTs Additional honorarium given by States/UTs (In \nRs.) \nAnganwadi Workers \n(AWW) \nAnganwadi Helper \n(AWH) \n1.  Andaman & Nicobar 3000 2500 \n2.  Andhra Pradesh 1200 700 \n3.  Arunachal Pradesh Nil Nil \n4.  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |937", "t": "Textile Mills Functioning", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 937 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019 \n \nTEXTILE MILLS FUNCTIONING \n \n937. SHRI BALAK NATH: \n        SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंरी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of textile mills functioning in the country at present, State-wise; \n \n(b) the number of textile mills closed down during the last three years and the current \nyear along with the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) the steps taken by the Government for the rehabilitation of the workers rendered \nunemployed due to closure of textile mills in the country; \n \n(d) whether the Government has received any representation with regard to the \nproblems being faced by the small and medium textile industries; and \n  \n(e) if so, the details thereof along with the steps taken by the Government in this \nregard? \n उत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र मंरी (श्रीमती स्मृतत ज़ूबिन इरानी) \nMINISTER OF TEXTILES \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a): As on 30.09.2019, there are 1366 operational Cotton / man-made fibre textile mills \nin the country. The State-wise number of operational mills is at Annexure-I. In addition, \nNational Textile Corporation (NTC) has 23 operational mills and five Joint Ventures (JV) as \nper details in Annexure-II. \n(b): Mills have been closed in the country, over the years mainly owing to financial \nproblems and labour related issues.  187 mills had closed during 2009 -10 to 2015 -16 \nperiod. During last three years i.e., 2016-17 to 2018-19, 41 cotton/man-made fibre textile \nmills have closed  in Non-Small Scale Industry (Non -SSI) sector.  In the current year, two \nmills have closed during April-September, 2019.  At the same time, 30 new mills have also \nenlisted with the Textile Commissioner, Mumbai during 2016 -17 and 2017-18.  The mills \nhave been closed in the country, mainly owing to financial problems and labour related \nissues. No mill was closed down by NTC during the last three years and current year. \n(c) to (e): Government of  India had introduced a Scheme v iz., Textile Workers \nRehabilitation Fund Scheme (TWRFS) with effect from 15.09.1986 to provide relief to the \nworkers, who were rendered jobless due to permanent closure of  Non-SSI textile mills in \nprivate sector. However, the scheme has now been merged with the  Rajiv Gandhi Shramik \nKalyan Yojana (RGSKY) of Ministry of Labour and Employment . Workers rendered jobless \ncan avail benefits under the said scheme. \n \n\n \nAnnexure-I \nState-wise Cotton / Man-Made Fibre Textile Mills  Non-Small Scale Industry (Non SSI) \n As on 30.09.2019 (Functional) \nSr. \nno. State No of Mills* \n1 Andhra Pradesh 107 \n2 Assam 0 \n3 Bihar 0 \n4 Chhattisgarh 0 \n5 Dadra Nagar Haveli 11 \n6 Daman & Diu 0 \n7 Delhi 0 \n8 Goa 1 \n9 Gujarat 53 \n10 Haryana 28 \n11 Himachal Pradesh 15 \n12 Jammu & Kashmir 1 \n13 Jharkhand 1 \n14 Karnataka 16 \n15 Kerala 17 \n16 Madhya Pradesh 38 \n17 Maharashtra 134 \n18 Manipur 0 \n19 Orissa 3 \n20 Pondicherry 8 \n21 Punjab 96 \n22 Rajasthan 38 \n23 Tamil Nadu 739 \n24 Telangana 26 \n25 Uttar Pradesh 14 \n26 Uttaranchal 6 \n27 West Bengal 14 \n  T o t a l 1366 \n \n(*As per the information maintained by the office of the Textile Commissioner, \nMumbai) \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATE-WISE LIST OF  28 NTC WORKING MILLS \n \nAnnexure-II \n    S.No. NAME OF THE  MILLS LOCATION PRESENT STATUS  \nANDHRA \nPRADESH     \n  \n1 TIRUPATHI COTTON MILLS   Renigunta OPERATIONAL  \nGUJARAT       \n2 RAJNAGAR TEXTILE MILLS Ahmedabad \nOPERATIONAL  \nKERALA       \n3 ALGAPPA TEX.MILLS Algappanagar OPERATIONAL  \n4 CANNANORE SPG.& WVG.MILLS Cannanore OPERATIONAL  \n5 KERALA LAXMI MILLS Trichur OPERATIONAL  \n6 VIJAYAMOHINI MILLS Trivandrum OPERATIONAL  \nKARNATAKA     \n7 NEW MINERVA MILLS HASSAN OPERATIONAL  \nMADHYA PRADESH     \n8 BURHANPUR TAPTI MILLS Burhanpur OPERATIONAL  \n9 NEW BHOPAL TEX. MILLS Bhopal OPERATIONAL  \nMAHARASHTRA     \n10 PODAR MILLS Mumbai OPERATIONAL  \n11 TATA MILLS Mumbai OPERATIONAL  \n12 INDIA UNITED M ILL NO.5 Mumbai OPERATIONAL  \n13 BARSHI TEX.MILLS Barshi OPERATIONAL  \n14 FINLAY MILLS  Achalpur OPERATIONAL  \nPUDUCHERRY     \n15 CANNANORE SPG.& WVG.MILLS Mahe OPERATIONAL  \nTAMIL NADU     \n16 CAMBODIA MILLS Coimbatore OPERATIONAL  \n17 COIMBATORE MURUGAN MILLS Coimbatore OPERATIONAL  \n18 PANKAJA MILLS Coimbatore OPERATIONAL  \n19 PIONEER SPINNERS MILLS Kamudakudi OPERATIONAL  \n20 SRI RANGAVILAS S.& W. MILLS Coimbatore OPERATIONAL  \n21 KALEESWARAR MILLS 'B' UNIT Kalayarkoil OPERATIONAL  \n22 COIMBATORE SPG & WVG MILLS  Coimbatore OPERATIONAL  \nWEST BENGAL       \n23 ARATI COTTON MILLS Dassnagar OPERATIONAL  \n(B)  5  NTC MILLS UNDER JOINT VENTURE   \nMAHARASHTRA     \n24 APOLLO TEX.MILLS Mumbai OPERATIONAL  \n25 GOLDMOHUR MILLS Mumbai OPERATIONAL  \n26 INDIA UNITED MILLS  NO.1 Mumbai \nFUNCTIONING WITH \nONLY TRADING \nACTIVITY \n27 NEW CITY OF BOMBAY MFG. MILLS Mumbai NON OPERATIONAL \n28 AURANGABAD TEXTILE MILLS Aurangabad NON OPERATIONAL \n***** \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|2|UNSTARRED |938", "t": "Pollution at Jabalpur", "head": "OIH \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUN-STARRED QUESTION N0. 938 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2019 \n \nPollution at Jabalpur \n \n938.  SHRI RAKESH SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the data received through Pollution Mapping Tools installed by the Central \nPollution Control Board in Jabalpur, Madhya Pradesh is collected online;  \n(b)  if so, the details of such data;  \n(c)  whether the level of pollution there is above the stipulated limits and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(d)  whether the Government has taken any remedial measures to deal with it; and  \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n(a) & (b) Madhya Pradesh Pollution Control Board has installed an online Continuous \nAmbient Air Quality Monitoring Stations (CAAQM) in Jabalpur and connected to \nCPCB in the month of September 2019. The data from September 17, 2019 to \nNovember 17, 2019 is given at Annexure -I.  \n \n(c) Earlier, the air quality data in Jabalpur was getting monitored manually and as per \nthe analysis of data annual average concentration of SO2 and NO2was within the \nNational Ambient Air Quality Standard (NAAQS) of 50µg/m3 and 40 µg/m3 \nrespectively. However, annual average concentration PM10and PM2.5during 2018 \nexceeded NAAQSof 60 µg/m3 and 40 µg/m3 respectively. \n \n(d) & (e) The Central Government has launched National Clean Air Programme \n(NCAP) under the Central Sector “Control of Pollution” Scheme as a time \nbound national level strategy to tackle air pollution problem across the country in \ncomprehensive manner. Based on ambient air quality data for the period 2011 – \n2015 and WHO report 2014/2018, 102 non-attainment cities including 6 cities \nnamely Bhopal, Dewas, Indore, Sagar, Ujjain, Gwalior fromMadhya Pradesh have \nbeen identified.States of non-attainment cities including Madhya Pradesh have been \ndirected to prepare and implement city specific Clean Air Action Plan. Further, the \nCentral Government has already notified 6 waste management rules covering solid \n\nwaste, plastic waste, e-waste, bio-medical waste, C&D waste and hazardous wastes \nin 2016.Notifications regarding dust mitigation measures for construction and \ndemolition activities issued. Emission standards for industrial sectors have been \nrevised from time to time.BS-IV standards adopted from 1st April, 2017. \nLeapfrogging from BS-IV to BS-VIfuel standards since 1st April, 2018 in NCT of \nDelhi, in NCR since October 2019 andfrom by 1st April, 2020 in the rest of the \ncountry for both fuel as well as vehicles. \n \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure -I. \n \nStateCityStationParameterAvgPeriodFromToPrescribed Standards 0-60 0-100 0-80 0-80 0-4 0-180 0-400From Date To DatePM2.5 PM10 NO2 SO2 CO Ozone NH3µg/m3µg/m3µg/m3µg/m3mg/m3µg/m3µg/m317-09-2019 00:00 18-09-2019 00:00 13.93 46.11 21.57 4.64 1.56 28.75 10.0818-09-2019 00:00 19-09-2019 00:00 14.86 26.7 15.1 2.44 1 23.01 9.5219-09-2019 00:00 20-09-2019 00:00 22.47 49.85 13.41 4.56 0.85 25.47 11.3620-09-2019 00:00 21-09-2019 00:00 14.51 34.88 13.94 4.91 0.99 16.83 10.0221-09-2019 00:00 22-09-2019 